UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO)'. The answer is A because the asymmetric division of oogenesis sends nearly all the cytoplasm and its organelles to the secondary oocyte, so the mitochondria the zygote inherits come from the egg alone. During oogenesis "the cytoplasm is divided unequally, and one daughter cell is much larger than the other...the secondary oocyte," while the polar body keeps almost none. Because "sperm contribute only DNA at fertilization—not cytoplasm," the text states that "the cytoplasm and all of the cytoplasmic organelles in the developing embryo are of egg-derived origin. This includes mitochondria," which were determined to be "maternally inherited." Chaining the asymmetric cytokinesis to the cytoplasm-only origin of the embryo's mitochondria explains why the child's mtDNA matches only the mother. (Choice A) Correct: the surviving gamete retains the cytoplasm and mitochondria, so all of the zygote's mtDNA is egg-derived and therefore maternal. (Choice B) Incorrect: the inequality of oogenesis is in cytoplasm, not chromosomes—each meiotic product receives "only one set of chromosomes," and mtDNA inheritance is unrelated to chromosome number. (Choice C) Incorrect: this reverses the partitioning—the secondary oocyte, not the polar body, keeps "the larger amount of cytoplasm," and the polar body simply degrades. (Choice D) Incorrect: sperm "contribute only DNA at fertilization—not cytoplasm," so no paternal mitochondria enter to be amplified, leaving the pool purely maternal. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires linking an observed sequencing result to the asymmetric cytoplasmic partitioning of oogenesis and the cytoplasm-only origin of embryonic organelles to predict why inheritance is strictly maternal.$px$ WHERE id = 'b61672e0-5a1f-4c47-a011-f0b5a1efbd00';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is A because the medullary respiratory center sets breathing rate through its rhythmic output; dropping the burst frequency from 15 to 9 per minute with unchanged tidal volume lowers minute (alveolar) ventilation by about 40%. Because CO2 is continuously produced by metabolism and cleared only by exhaling alveolar gas, cutting the rate of exhalation below the rate of production causes CO2 to accumulate, so arterial (and alveolar) CO2 rises.

(Choice A) is correct: alveolar ventilation = (tidal volume − dead space) × rate. With tidal volume and dead space fixed, dropping the rate from 15 to 9 per minute (a ~40% reduction) reduces alveolar ventilation, so CO2 is removed more slowly than it is generated and its partial pressure climbs — the same mechanism behind hypoventilation-driven hypercapnia.

(Choice B) is incorrect: the notion that slower breaths let each alveolus 'unload more' inverts the physiology. Alveolar–capillary CO2 equilibration is fast and essentially complete during a normal breath; total CO2 clearance is governed by how much fresh air is moved per minute, and fewer breaths move less air, raising CO2 rather than lowering it.

(Choice C) is incorrect: ventilation depends on both rate and tidal volume. Holding tidal volume constant does not hold minute ventilation constant when rate falls; the reduced number of breaths per minute still decreases total alveolar ventilation and therefore changes CO2.

(Choice D) is incorrect: this invents a routing effect. The rhythmic rate of the diaphragm does not reassign it to the 'venous' side of the pulmonary circuit; gas exchange occurs at the alveolar–capillary membrane regardless of firing frequency, and the pulmonary vessels' oxygenation status is not set by respiratory rate.

Skill: This item requires reasoning from a described change in physiological drive to a quantitative gas-exchange consequence rather than recalling a definition.$px$ WHERE id = 'b61b54a9-e07c-4a07-906e-ac09636b3014';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is C because the spindle microtubules are the structures that physically attach to chromosomes and pull the separated sister chromatids toward opposite poles; if they cannot attach at the kinetochores, that segregating force is lost and the chromatids are not partitioned equally between the two forming daughter cells. The text states that microtubules "pull replicated chromosomes to opposite ends of a dividing cell," identifying them as the cytoskeletal element responsible for chromosome segregation (lines 5908–5909). Disabling only the kinetochore attachment—while leaving centrosomes, nuclear breakdown, and tubulin polymerization functional—isolates the spindle's pulling role, so the predictable result is missegregation, not a structural change to chromatin or a switch to a different filament system. (Choice A) Histone–DNA association and chromatin condensation are independent of spindle attachment; the drug targets microtubule binding, not the histone-packaged chromosome, so the chromosomes remain condensed and visible. (Choice B) Microfilaments are actin-based and function in cytoplasmic streaming and cytokinesis, not in poleward chromosome movement; they do not substitute for spindle microtubules in pulling chromatids. (Choice C) Correct: with no microtubule–kinetochore attachment, the replicated sister chromatids cannot be drawn to opposite poles, so daughter cells receive unequal, incomplete chromosome sets. (Choice D) Centrosomes are microtubule-organizing centers, not an independent force generator; they cannot accelerate segregation when the very microtubules that connect to the chromosomes are detached. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must predict the downstream consequence of selectively disrupting the spindle's attachment to chromosomes rather than simply recalling what microtubules are.$px$ WHERE id = 'b6b7bc1a-6a17-4c03-a661-5fa4eddfa70d';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is C because sequences I and III each place \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release before the power stroke and the power stroke before \(\text{ADP}\) release, matching the canonical cycle order, whereas II reverses the cycle by putting \(\text{ATP}\)-driven detachment before the power stroke. Cross-bridge formation occurs when the cocked myosin head attaches to actin while \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) are still bound; \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) is then released, causing a stronger attachment, after which the head moves toward the M-line in the power stroke, pulling actin about 10 nm. After the power stroke, \(\text{ADP}\) is released while actin and myosin remain bound, and only then does a new \(\text{ATP}\) attach to cause detachment, followed by \(\text{ATP}\) hydrolysis that re-cocks the head. (Choice A) Sequence I is correctly ordered, but II is not, because \(\text{ATP}\) binding/detachment does not precede \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release and a power stroke—detachment is followed by hydrolysis and re-cocking, and the head must rebind actin before any new power stroke. (Choice B) II alone is incorrect because it reverses the cycle: \(\text{ATP}\) binding causes detachment only after the power stroke and \(\text{ADP}\) release, so detachment cannot be immediately followed by \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release and a stroke. (Choice C) is correct because both I and III preserve the canonical order \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release → power stroke → \(\text{ADP}\) release, with III beginning one step earlier at attachment of the \(\text{ADP}\)/\(\text{P}_\text{i}\)-loaded cocked head. (Choice D) includes the reversed sequence II; although III is correctly ordered, II misplaces \(\text{ATP}\)-driven detachment before the power stroke, so the combination is wrong. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of the cross-bridge cycle to order specific molecular and mechanical events within a single situation.$px$ WHERE id = 'b769501b-3be8-4def-a7a8-7cc278bc9d1a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. The answer to this question is D because three pieces of evidence must be chained. First, reactivity (rate of tarnishing) is governed by how easily the FIRST valence electron leaves, i.e. by \(\text{IE}_1\): \(\text{X}\) has \(\text{IE}_1 = 496\ \text{kJ/mol}\) versus \(738\ \text{kJ/mol}\) for \(\text{Y}\), so \(\text{X}\) loses its first electron more readily and tarnishes faster — \(\text{X}\) is the Group 1 alkali metal (\([\text{Ne}]3s^1\), sodium) and \(\text{Y}\) is the Group 2 alkaline earth metal (\([\text{Ne}]3s^2\), magnesium). Second, the stable cation is set by the noble-gas core, which is flagged by the large jump in successive ionization energy: for \(\text{X}\), the jump is between \(\text{IE}_1\) (496) and \(\text{IE}_2\) (4562, a roughly ninefold rise), showing only one electron lies outside the \([\text{Ne}]\) core, so \(\text{X}\) stops at \(\text{X}^+\). Third, the energy to reach that stable cation is therefore just \(\text{IE}_1 = 496\ \text{kJ/mol}\). Thus the more reactive metal forms the lower-charge ion, and only \(\text{IE}_1\) is summed. (Choice A) correctly identifies \(\text{X}\) as faster-tarnishing but mismatches the charge and the energy: it assigns \(\text{X}\) a \(2+\) charge yet uses \(738\ \text{kJ/mol}\), which is \(\text{Y}\)'s \(\text{IE}_1\), so it both overstates \(\text{X}\)'s stable charge past its \([\text{Ne}]\) core and pulls the energy value from the wrong element. (Choice B) keeps the correct, more-reactive metal \(\text{X}\) but incorrectly ties ease of first ionization to a higher charge, adding \(\text{IE}_1 + \text{IE}_2 = 496 + 4562 = 5058\ \text{kJ/mol}\) to reach \(\text{X}^{2+}\); removing that second electron breaks into the filled \([\text{Ne}]\) core, which the ninefold ionization jump shows is prohibitively costly, so \(\text{X}\) does not form \(\text{X}^{2+}\) under these conditions. (Choice C) reverses the reactivity relationship by assuming the metal able to release more electrons, \(\text{Y}\), is the more reactive one; \(\text{Y}\)'s higher \(\text{IE}_1\) of 738 makes it tarnish more slowly, and although \(2189\ \text{kJ/mol} = 738 + 1451\) is the correct energy for \(\text{Y}\) to reach \(\text{Y}^{2+}\), \(\text{Y}\) is not the faster-tarnishing metal the stem asks about. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the periodic ionization-energy trend, read the successive-ionization jump to locate each metal's noble-gas core, and sum only the ionization steps that precede that core to compute the energy to the correct stable cation.$px$ WHERE id = 'b7858478-c39f-4aeb-bb6b-32894e259ab7';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth.' Gas exchange at the placenta follows the same principle as gas exchange in the lung: it occurs by diffusion, a process in which transport is "driven by a concentration gradient," so gas molecules "move from a region of high concentration to a region of low concentration," and "the rate of diffusion of a gas is proportional to its partial pressure." In utero the fetal lungs are compressed, filled with amniotic fluid, and nonfunctional, so the placenta—which "functions in gas exchange and waste management"—is the site of respiration; maternal blood (\(P_{O_2}\) ~50 mmHg) is more oxygen-rich than the returning fetal blood (\(P_{O_2}\) ~20 mmHg), establishing the gradient that loads oxygen into the fetal blood leaving in the umbilical vein (\(P_{O_2}\) ~30 mmHg). (Choice A) is correct: oxygen crosses the placental barrier by passive diffusion down its partial-pressure gradient, from the higher-\(P_{O_2}\) maternal blood to the lower-\(P_{O_2}\) fetal blood, exactly as the data show. (Choice B) is incorrect: while the placenta does use active, ATP-dependent transport for some solutes such as certain amino acids, oxygen is small and lipid-soluble and moves passively down its gradient; no pump is needed, and the maternal-to-fetal direction here is with the gradient, not against it. (Choice C) is incorrect: the fetal lungs are fluid-filled and nonfunctional before birth and perform no gas exchange, and the umbilical vein carries oxygenated blood toward the fetus, not toward the placenta for storage. (Choice D) is incorrect: maternal immunoglobulins do cross the placenta to provide passive immunity, but antibodies do not transport oxygen; oxygen carriage in blood is a function of hemoglobin within red blood cells, not of immunoglobulins. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that gases diffuse down their partial-pressure gradient to interpret the measured \(P_{O_2}\) values and identify the transport mechanism.$px$ WHERE id = 'b7acaa10-34e9-431e-96e0-7f5b5477e141';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is C because the voltage-gated Na+ channels open only once the depolarizing membrane reaches the threshold of about -55 mV. A graded depolarizing stimulus drives the membrane from its resting -70 mV up toward -55 mV, and once the membrane reaches that voltage—the threshold potential of -55 mV—the voltage-gated Na+ channels open, with the activation gate opening as the membrane potential crosses -55 mV. Any depolarization that fails to bring the membrane to -55 mV does not reach threshold and does not open these channels. (Choice A) -70 mV is the resting potential, where the activation gate is still closed and no voltage-gated Na+ channels have opened. (Choice B) A voltage more negative than -70 mV is hyperpolarization, which moves the membrane away from threshold rather than toward channel opening. (Choice C) About -55 mV is correct, because this is the threshold the depolarizing membrane must reach for the voltage-gated Na+ channels to open. (Choice D) About +30 mV is the peak of depolarization reached after the channels are already open and Na+ has rushed in, not the voltage that triggers their opening. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the specific membrane voltage—threshold at about -55 mV—at which voltage-gated Na+ channels open.$px$ WHERE id = 'b81deb6b-dffe-49f6-a74f-289579627f1a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is D because proteins are classified at the tertiary level by overall 3D shape into two broad architectures: fibrous proteins adopt elongated structures that serve structural roles (e.g., collagen and α-keratin, the major component of hair, skin, and nails), while globular proteins fold into a roughly spherical, compact form (e.g., myoglobin, which binds \(\text{O}_2\)). Large fibrous proteins are not soluble in water, whereas globular proteins are compact with water excluded from their interiors, leaving polar groups on the soluble surface. Keratin's elongated, insoluble filaments make it fibrous, and myoglobin's compact, soluble fold makes it globular. (Choice A) reverses the assignment: keratin is the fibrous protein and myoglobin is the globular one, not the opposite. (Choice B) is wrong because myoglobin is not fibrous or insoluble — it is a compact, water-soluble globular protein. (Choice C) is wrong because keratin is not globular or freely soluble — large fibrous proteins such as keratin are insoluble in water. (Choice D) is correct: keratin is an elongated, insoluble fibrous protein, and myoglobin is a compact, soluble globular protein. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the distinction between fibrous (elongated, structural, insoluble) and globular (compact, soluble) protein architectures and correctly classify each example.$px$ WHERE id = 'b849cc6e-21d4-497b-a6a0-ac45ce1fe77b';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$Among all the aldohexoses, β-D-glucose is by far the most abundant in nature, and its six-membered ring is unusually resistant to distortion into higher-energy ring shapes. The structure shown is the favored chair conformation of β-D-glucopyranose. Reasoning from the positions the ring substituents occupy in the structure shown, which feature best explains why this particular sugar is thermodynamically favored over other aldohexoses?

[FIGURE: The 4C1 chair conformation of β-D-glucopyranose, drawn in the standard zig-zag chair form with the ring oxygen at the upper right and the six ring carbons alternating between "up" and "down" vertices. At every ring carbon, two bonds leave the ring atom in addition to its two ring bonds: one bond is drawn pointing nearly straight up or straight down, parallel to the vertical axis running through the middle of the ring, and the other is drawn splaying out sideways away from the ring. In this drawing, each near-vertical bond carries a hydrogen atom, while the four ring –OH groups (on C1, C2, C3, C4) together with the C5 –CH2OH are each drawn on the sideways-splayed bond at their carbon.]$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is B because the stability of a pyranose ring is governed by how its substituents are distributed between the two kinds of positions on a chair: those on bonds that splay outward from the ring (equatorial) and those on bonds that run parallel to the vertical ring axis (axial). A substituent forced onto an axial bond on one face is crowded against the two other axial groups on that same face two carbons away, producing destabilizing 1,3-diaxial strain. Reading the structure shown, each near-vertical (axial) bond carries only a small hydrogen, while every one of the four ring –OH groups and the –CH2OH sits on an outward-splaying bond — that is, they all occupy equatorial positions. With no bulky group forced axial, there is no 1,3-diaxial crowding, so this conformation sits at an energy minimum. β-D-glucose is the only aldohexose whose favored chair can place all of its bulky substituents equatorial, which is precisely why it is the most stable and most abundant aldohexose in nature.

(Choice B) is correct: it reads the drawing correctly — the bulky groups are on the outward-splaying (equatorial) bonds — and ties that placement to relief of same-face 1,3-diaxial crowding.

(Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the geometry. The figure shows the bulky groups on the outward-splaying bonds, not on the near-vertical bonds that run parallel to the ring axis. Groups on those axial bonds would be forced together as 1,3-diaxial neighbors, which maximizes strain rather than lowering the energy.

(Choice C) is incorrect: this describes an anomeric-type orbital interaction, which would if anything favor the α-anomer (axial anomeric OH), not β-D-glucose, and it addresses only one carbon. It cannot explain the overall stability advantage, which comes from the arrangement of all five bulky substituents seen in the figure, not from the anomeric position alone.

(Choice D) is incorrect: it is factually wrong. All D-aldohexoses (glucose, mannose, galactose) have the same number of chiral centers — four — so glucose does not have 'one fewer.' Its stability advantage comes from where its substituents sit in the chair, not from having fewer stereocenters.

At the Skill 2 level, the task is not to recall a memorized fact but to interpret the given conformation: you must read that the bulky substituents lie on the outward-splaying bonds while only hydrogens occupy the axial bonds, recognize that as the all-equatorial arrangement, and connect it to minimized 1,3-diaxial strain — the reasoning chain that explains β-D-glucose's exceptional prevalence.$px$ WHERE id = 'b88b6908-c40c-4cdf-a11c-d54b3d502f6a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separations and Purifications.' The column described is a size-exclusion (gel-filtration) column: molecules too large for the bead pores are excluded from the internal volume and travel through the void volume around the beads, so they elute quickly, whereas smaller molecules that enter the pores follow a longer path and elute later. Elution order therefore runs from largest to smallest. Ranking the three proteins by mass gives thyroglobulin (669 kDa) > ovalbumin (44 kDa) > ribonuclease A (14 kDa), so ovalbumin elutes second—after the excluded thyroglobulin and before the smaller ribonuclease A.

The answer is A because ovalbumin (44 kDa) is the intermediate-sized protein: it partly enters the pores (it is below the ~80 kDa cutoff) but is larger than ribonuclease A, so it accesses less of the internal pore volume and travels a shorter path than the smallest protein. Thyroglobulin (669 kDa) exceeds the pore-admission limit, is excluded, and elutes first; ribonuclease A (14 kDa) samples the pores most extensively and elutes last. Ovalbumin thus appears in the middle fraction.

(Choice A) is correct: it places ovalbumin after the largest protein (thyroglobulin, excluded and fastest) and before the smallest (ribonuclease A, most retained and slowest), matching the largest-to-smallest elution order.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it claims ovalbumin leaves before both others, which would require ovalbumin to be the largest species. Thyroglobulin (669 kDa) is far larger, is excluded from the pores, and necessarily elutes ahead of ovalbumin, so ovalbumin cannot be first.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it claims ovalbumin leaves after both others, which would require ovalbumin to be the smallest species that samples the pores most extensively. Ribonuclease A (14 kDa) is smaller, takes the longest path, and elutes after ovalbumin, so ovalbumin cannot be last. This choice reflects the reversed assumption that small molecules elute first.

(Choice D) is incorrect: co-elution with thyroglobulin would require both proteins to be excluded from the pores and travel only through the void volume. Ovalbumin (44 kDa) is below the ~80 kDa admission limit, so it does enter the pores and is retarded relative to the excluded thyroglobulin; the two cannot leave together.

This question requires Skill 2 reasoning: rather than recalling a definition, you must apply the size-exclusion elution principle to the given molecular weights and pore cutoff to predict where the intermediate-sized protein appears in the fraction sequence.$px$ WHERE id = 'b938ec39-84e0-4f91-9a2b-fc6e66bb2ecf';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is D because the \(\text{pI}\) is found by averaging the two \(\text{pK}_a\) values that flank the net-neutral species. Arginine is a basic amino acid with three ionizable groups (\(\alpha\text{-COOH} = 2.0\), \(\alpha\text{-}\text{NH}_3^+ = 9.0\), guanidinium \(= 12.5\)); as pH rises the \(\alpha\text{-COOH}\) deprotonates first (giving the net-neutral zwitterion), and the next group to lose a proton is the \(\alpha\text{-}\text{NH}_3^+\). The \(\text{pI}\) is obtained by averaging the \(\text{pK}_a\) values of the two groups closest to and on either side of the net-neutral species, so for this basic residue you average \(\alpha\text{-}\text{NH}_3^+\) and the guanidinium side chain: \(\text{pI} = (9.0 + 12.5)/2 = 10.75 \approx 10.8\). (Choice A) is incorrect because \(\text{pI} \approx 5.5\) comes from averaging the two lowest \(\text{pK}_a\) values, \((2.0 + 9.0)/2\) — the pair that flanks the neutral species of a residue WITHOUT a basic side chain, not arginine. (Choice B) is incorrect because \(\text{pI} \approx 7.3\) comes from averaging the extreme \(\text{pK}_a\) values, \((2.0 + 12.5)/2\), which skips over the central \(\alpha\text{-}\text{NH}_3^+\) group rather than using the two that actually straddle the zwitterion. (Choice C) is incorrect because \(\text{pI} \approx 12.5\) simply reports the guanidinium \(\text{pK}_a\) alone without averaging it with the adjacent group, ignoring that a \(\text{pI}\) is always a midpoint between two \(\text{pK}_a\) values. (Choice D) is correct: averaging the \(\alpha\text{-}\text{NH}_3^+\) (9.0) and guanidinium (12.5) \(\text{pK}_a\) values gives \(\text{pI} \approx 10.8\), consistent with arginine being a strongly basic amino acid. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the pI-averaging rule to a specific pKa set, identify which two of three pKa values flank the net-neutral species, and compute the result.$px$ WHERE id = 'b95b3c51-7f31-45a4-86f0-108a5db8631d';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$At the placenta, oxygen in maternal blood sits at a partial pressure of roughly 35–50 mm Hg while fetal blood arriving at the placenta is at only about 20 mm Hg, leaving a modest pressure gradient to drive oxygen across the chorion. A physiologist asks how the fetus still manages to load adequate oxygen across so shallow a gradient. Which mechanism BEST accounts for this?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is A because fetal hemoglobin (HbF) has an intrinsically left-shifted (higher-affinity) dissociation curve, so it loads oxygen efficiently even at the low partial pressures found in placental blood. The text states that maternal placental pO2 is "about 35 to 50 mm Hg" while fetal blood at the placenta is "about 20 mm Hg," so "there is not as much diffusion of oxygen into the fetal blood supply," and the fetus "overcomes this problem by having a greater affinity for oxygen than maternal hemoglobin" owing to two structurally distinct subunits. Because of that higher affinity, HbF reaches substantial saturation at the low ~20 mm Hg prevailing in the placenta and keeps accepting oxygen unloaded by maternal hemoglobin even though the maternal-to-fetal gradient is shallow. (Choice A) is correct: a left-shifted, high-affinity HbF curve loads oxygen well at low pO2, compensating for the small gradient. (Choice B) is wrong because fetal placental pO2 (~20 mm Hg) is lower, not higher, than maternal (~35–50 mm Hg), so the gradient is small, not steep. (Choice C) is wrong because a rightward Bohr shift lowers affinity and promotes oxygen dissociation/unloading, the opposite of what efficient loading requires; HbF's advantage comes from its intrinsically left-shifted curve. (Choice D) is wrong because elevated BPG actually reduces hemoglobin's oxygen affinity by promoting dissociation, and the text attributes HbF's higher affinity to its different subunit structure, not to BPG. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires combining the quantitative pO2 data with the mechanism of the oxygen–hemoglobin dissociation curve to predict how oxygen loading succeeds despite a shallow gradient.$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"Fetal hemoglobin's left-shifted dissociation curve gives it high affinity, so it becomes substantially saturated even at the low ~20 mm Hg present in placental blood and keeps drawing oxygen released by maternal hemoglobin.","label":"A"},{"text":"Fetal placental pO2 (~20 mm Hg) is actually higher than maternal placental pO2 (~35–50 mm Hg), so a steep gradient drives oxygen rapidly into fetal blood despite the small difference in values.","label":"B"},{"text":"The low pO2 and high CO2 of fetal blood shift the fetal hemoglobin curve to the right via the Bohr effect, making fetal hemoglobin pick up oxygen more readily at the placenta.","label":"C"},{"text":"Fetal erythrocytes carry far more 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate than maternal cells, which raises fetal hemoglobin's oxygen affinity and pulls oxygen across the placenta into fetal blood.","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'b9b0bebe-4fb5-4ee2-898b-3dcf0cfd2b34';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is D because an unchanged \(V_{max}\) paired with an increased apparent \(K_m\) is the defining kinetic signature of competitive inhibition: with a competitive inhibitor \(V_{max}\) does not change while the apparent \(K_m\) rises. Mechanistically, the inhibitor and substrate both bind to the same site on the enzyme, so they compete for the active site; as \([S]\) rises to infinity all enzyme is driven into ES, no free E remains for I to bind, and \(V_{max}\) is not changed; meanwhile I binding to free E shifts E + S \(\rightarrow\) ES to the left, which increases the apparent \(K_m\). Only a signature that raises \(K_m\) yet spares \(V_{max}\) fits, and that is unique to the competitive case. (Choice A) is wrong because noncompetitive inhibition lowers \(V_{max}\) while leaving \(K_m\) unchanged: the apparent \(V_{max}\) is less than the real \(V_{max}\), the apparent \(K_m\) does not change, and only \(V_{max}\) decreases — the observed unchanged \(V_{max}\) rules it out. (Choice B) is wrong because uncompetitive inhibition, where I binds only to ES and not to free E, decreases both parameters: in the presence of I, both \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\) decrease, so it cannot leave \(V_{max}\) unchanged and would lower rather than raise \(K_m\). (Choice C) is wrong because the three reversible types modeled here are, by definition, reversible, and irreversible inhibition is not characterized by this reversible, substrate-relievable \(V_{max}\)-unchanged/\(K_m\)-increased pattern; it is a distinct category that does not produce the described signature. (Choice D) is correct: an unchanged \(V_{max}\) with an elevated apparent \(K_m\) matches competitive inhibition exactly. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the diagnostic \(K_m\)/\(V_{max}\) signatures to classify the inhibition type directly from the assay data rather than recall a single definition.$px$ WHERE id = 'bae9a29f-286a-45d4-880b-a78e10922d09';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is D because the only horizontal gene transfer mechanism that requires neither cell-to-cell contact nor a viral vector is transformation, the uptake of free DNA from the environment. The filter blocks every cell from crossing, so any process needing physical contact is ruled out, and the medium is phage-free, so any process needing a bacteriophage is ruled out; in transformation, a prokaryote takes up naked DNA found in its environment—DNA derived from other cells that have lysed on death and released their contents, including their genome, into the surroundings—and competent cells actively bind this environmental DNA and transport it across their cell envelopes into their cytoplasm. Because only purified, dissolved DNA can cross the filter, that DNA carries the resistance allele to the recipient, which recombines it into its genome. (Choice A) Conjugation is incorrect: DNA is directly transferred from one prokaryote to another by means of a conjugation pilus, which brings the organisms into contact, but the filter prevents the cells from ever touching. (Choice B) Generalized transduction is incorrect because it requires a viral vector: viruses that infect bacteria (bacteriophages) can move short pieces of chromosomal DNA from one bacterium to another in a process called transduction, yet the medium is confirmed phage-free. (Choice C) Transposition moves an element within or between DNA molecules inside a cell, not across an open medium between separated cells, so it cannot deliver the gene across the filter. (Choice D) is correct because transformation is the lone mechanism in which naked DNA is taken up from the environment, needing no contact and no phage. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the two experimental constraints—no cell contact and no phage—to eliminate conjugation, transduction, and transposition and identify transformation as the only viable route.$px$ WHERE id = 'bb25c047-c853-4732-b409-4d2563d04955';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A single Lewis structure of the carbonate ion \(\text{CO}_3^{2-}\) shows one C=O double bond and two C–O single bonds. Considering all equivalent resonance contributors, which prediction about the three carbon–oxygen bonds is correct?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because resonance delocalizes the double bond equally over all three positions, so each C–O bond is an average of a single and a double bond and therefore lies intermediate in length between the two. The carbonate ion has three equivalent resonance contributors, each placing the C=O double bond on a different oxygen; the true structure is the resonance hybrid in which the extra bonding pair is shared equally, giving every bond a length and strength between those of a pure single bond and a pure double bond. (Choice B) Treating every bond as a full double bond ignores that only one double bond's worth of pi electron density is distributed across three positions, so no bond reaches full double-bond character. (Choice C) Predicting two short and one long bond corresponds to taking a single static Lewis structure literally, whereas the equivalence of the resonance contributors makes all three positions identical. (Choice D) Treating every bond as a full single bond neglects the shared pi pair entirely, which would leave the delocalized double-bond character unaccounted for. Skill 2$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"All three bonds are identical, each shorter than a C–O single bond but longer than a C=O double bond.","label":"A"},{"text":"All three bonds are identical, each equal in length to a full C=O double bond.","label":"B"},{"text":"Two bonds are short like C=O double bonds and one is long like a C–O single bond.","label":"C"},{"text":"All three bonds are identical, each equal in length to a full C–O single bond.","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'bb331ef7-f593-40b8-9798-4f86ac38d12f';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This Organic Chemistry item falls under the content category 'Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules'. The answer to this question is B because \(\text{CH}_3\text{C(=O)NHCH}_3\) contains the amide linkage — an acyl carbonyl carbon (\(\text{C=O}\)) bonded to nitrogen — and that nitrogen carries a methyl group in addition to one hydrogen; the alkyl group sitting directly on the amide nitrogen is what makes it N-substituted (a secondary amide). (Choice A) \(\text{CH}_3\text{C(=O)NH}_2\) is also an amide, but its nitrogen bears only hydrogens, so it is an unsubstituted (primary) amide rather than an N-substituted one. (Choice C) \(\text{CH}_3\text{C(=O)OCH}_3\) places the alkyl group on oxygen; a carbonyl bonded to \(\text{-OR}\) is an ester, which has no amide nitrogen at all. (Choice D) \(\text{CH}_3\text{CH}_2\text{NHCH}_3\) has an alkyl-bearing nitrogen but lacks an adjacent \(\text{C=O}\); without the carbonyl it is a secondary amine, not an amide. Recognizing the amide carbonyl–nitrogen linkage and locating an alkyl substituent specifically on that nitrogen makes this a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question.$px$ WHERE id = 'bb3756c3-c318-4310-a4fd-49d7f49273e3';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is A because bile salts have two opposite effects at the droplet surface. Emulsification is helpful: by dispersing fat into small droplets, bile salts create the large lipid–water interface lipase acts on. But because bile salts themselves coat that interface, they crowd it and can knock lipase off the very surface it needs to bind — a hindrance to lipase's contact with substrate. Colipase resolves this tension: it binds both the droplet interface and lipase, tethering lipase to the fat surface despite the bile-salt coating. Thus the emulsifying benefit is preserved while the interfacial blocking is overcome. Without colipase, the same bile salts that enabled emulsification would keep lipase from binding, so their net effect at the surface would impair, not aid, lipase action.

(Choice A) is correct: it captures both faces of bile-salt action (helpful dispersal, harmful interfacial displacement) and colipase's role as the anchor that lets lipase bind despite the bile-salt coating.

(Choice B) is incorrect: bile salts do not denature lipase, and colipase is not a refolding chaperone. Colipase acts by physically anchoring native lipase to the lipid interface, not by restoring a denatured enzyme's fold.

(Choice C) is incorrect: colipase does not act as a pH buffer, and the problem bile salts create is spatial occupancy of the interface, not a pH shift. This choice invents a pH mechanism unrelated to the interfacial competition described.

(Choice D) is incorrect: bile salts do not hydrolyze triacylglycerol at all — their action is physical emulsification, and hydrolysis is done by lipase. There is no competing bile-salt hydrolysis for colipase to block.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, this asks you to reconcile the dual (helpful and hindering) roles of bile salts at an interface and infer why an anchoring cofactor is required, rather than recall colipase's name.$px$ WHERE id = 'bb584799-0209-4f24-9e98-f5834d9c82b5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Atoms, nuclear decay, electronic structure, and atomic chemical behavior.' In the Bohr model the energy levels are quantized as \(E_n = -k/n^2\), so the spacing between adjacent levels is not uniform: it is enormous near the nucleus (between \(n=1\) and \(n=2\)) and shrinks rapidly toward zero as \(n\) grows. The energy of an emitted photon equals \(|\Delta E| = k\left(\tfrac{1}{n_f^2} - \tfrac{1}{n_i^2}\right)\), so the transition ending at the lowest final level dominates, regardless of how many levels the electron drops through. (Choice A) is correct: ending at \(n_f=1\) taps the largest gap in the atom, giving \(|\Delta E| = k(1 - \tfrac{1}{9}) \approx 1.94\times10^{-18}\ \text{J}\) — roughly four times any of the others, so this photon is the most energetic. (Choice B) is incorrect: this transition spans the largest change in level number (\(\Delta n = 3\)), which tempts students to pick it, but both levels lie high up where the spacing is tiny, giving only \(|\Delta E| \approx 1.82\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}\) — the lowest energy of the four. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(k(\tfrac{1}{4} - \tfrac{1}{25}) \approx 4.58\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}\); ending at \(n=2\) is far weaker than ending at \(n=1\), so despite the large drop this photon carries only about a quarter of the key's energy. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(k(\tfrac{1}{4} - \tfrac{1}{16}) \approx 4.09\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}\); like Choice C it terminates at \(n=2\), so it cannot rival a transition that terminates at \(n=1\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the \(E_n = -k/n^2\) relationship to recognize that non-uniform level spacing—not the number of levels crossed—determines the emitted photon energy.$px$ WHERE id = 'bb9a71d8-55fb-4b78-bda8-9cb8a454bec1';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is D because the relative refractory period coincides with hyperpolarization, when the membrane voltage is transiently more negative than the -70 mV resting potential. As repolarization finishes, the slow-closing voltage-gated K+ channels let K+ continue to leave the cell, so the diffusion of K+ "continues for a short period of time past the time of the achievement of the resting potential, and the membrane hyperpolarizes, in that the membrane potential becomes more negative than the cell's normal resting potential" (Biology 2e). During this window "the hyperpolarized membrane is in a refractory period," and a reading of -80 mV — below the roughly -70 mV rest — is the only value consistent with that hyperpolarized state. The relative period is also when "only ... a stronger stimulus" can fire the cell because outward K+ flow opposes depolarization (Anatomy & Physiology 2e). (Choice A) +30 mV is the peak of depolarization driven by Na+ influx, which falls in the absolute refractory period when Na+ channels are inactivated, not the relative period. (Choice B) 0 mV would imply the membrane has no charge separation, but the membrane is never charge-neutral during recovery; it is repolarizing and then hyperpolarizing past rest. (Choice C) -45 mV is above threshold and more positive than the resting potential, describing a depolarized membrane rather than the hyperpolarized state of the relative refractory period. (Choice D) -80 mV reflects the membrane driven more negative than rest by ongoing K+ efflux, the defining signature of the relative refractory period. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires interpreting recorded voltage data and reasoning from the underlying ion-flux mechanism to identify which value matches the hyperpolarized relative refractory period.$px$ WHERE id = 'bbba957f-a340-4f9c-9cae-f228333fe4d6';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is D because the data drop by a fixed 20 mg/dL each hour (150 to 130 to 110 to 90 to 70) — a constant absolute rate of decline that does not slow as the concentration falls. A constant amount removed per unit time, independent of the remaining concentration, is the defining signature of zero-order kinetics, which arises when the metabolizing enzyme is saturated and operating at \(V_{max}\). The correct reading must be derived from the equal successive differences in the table, not recalled.

(Choice D) is correct: equal 20 mg/dL decrements per hour show a concentration-independent (zero-order) rate, consistent with a saturated enzyme running at maximal velocity.
(Choice A) is incorrect: first-order elimination removes a constant fraction, which would give shrinking absolute drops (e.g., 150 to 105 to 73.5); the table's constant absolute decrements rule this out even though ethanol is a classic saturation example.
(Choice B) is incorrect: the decline is linear, not steepening, so nothing in the data supports a rate rising with the square of concentration — this misreads a straight line as an accelerating curve.
(Choice C) is incorrect: it pairs the right order label (zero-order) with the wrong justification — a constant fraction and rate proportional to remaining ethanol describe first-order behavior, so the description contradicts the label and the data.

At the Skill 4 (data-based reasoning) level, the student must extract the pattern of successive differences from the table to assign the kinetic order and connect it to enzyme saturation, rather than recognizing a stated fact.$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"First-order — a constant fraction is removed per hour, indicating the enzyme is far below saturation so rate is proportional to ethanol concentration","label":"A"},{"text":"Second-order — the decline steepens over time, indicating the rate depends on the square of the ethanol concentration","label":"B"},{"text":"Zero-order — the concentration falls by a constant fraction per hour, indicating the enzyme rate is proportional to how much ethanol remains","label":"C"},{"text":"Zero-order — the concentration falls by a constant amount per hour, indicating the metabolizing enzyme is saturated so rate is independent of ethanol concentration","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'bbbd8c65-af09-4ff5-969e-ac75f7ca7300';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.' The CNS contains four glial types—astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, ependymal cells, and microglia—but only microglia are thought to originate from the immune-cell (macrophage/leukocyte) lineage rather than from the neuroectoderm that produces the other glia. Microglia are believed to arise from white blood cells (macrophages) that migrate into the CNS during early development; leukocytes that leave the bloodstream are given different names depending on their function, and those that take up residence in the CNS are called microglia, which is why microglia are described as CNS-resident macrophages. The lineage clue in the stem—descent from the blood-borne macrophage line rather than the neural tube—singles out microglia. (Choice A) is incorrect: astrocytes are neural-tube-derived support cells that maintain the extracellular chemical environment and contribute to the blood-brain barrier; their described function is real, but they do not share the immune-cell lineage the stem specifies. (Choice B) is incorrect: ependymal cells are neuroectoderm-derived, line the ventricles, and filter blood at the choroid plexus to make cerebrospinal fluid, a fluid-handling role with a non-immune origin. (Choice C) is incorrect: oligodendrocytes arise from neural precursors and insulate CNS axons in myelin; the described insulation function is accurate but does not match the blood-borne origin. (Choice D) is correct: microglia are the CNS glia derived from the blood-borne immune-cell lineage and serve as the nervous system's resident immune cells that perform surveillance and clear debris, matching the origin described in the stem. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the distinct developmental origin and immune identity that separate microglia from the other three CNS glial cell types.$px$ WHERE id = 'bbf847a0-d12b-449c-bc4f-22764e8b81cd';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Citric Acid Cycle.'

The answer is C because the carrier choice is dictated by thermodynamics. Electrons flow spontaneously from a donor to \(\text{NAD}^+\) only when the donor couple sits at a more negative reduction potential than the \(\text{NAD}^+/\text{NADH}\) couple (near \(-0.32\ \text{V}\)). The succinate/fumarate couple sits near \(0\ \text{V}\) — well above \(\text{NAD}^+/\text{NADH}\) — so it cannot reduce \(\text{NAD}^+\). \(\text{FAD}\), tightly bound in succinate dehydrogenase, accepts these electrons instead. That same enzyme is Complex II, embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane, which is why this step is the direct membrane link between the cycle and the electron transport chain.

(Choice C) is correct: because succinate/fumarate is not negative enough to reduce \(\text{NAD}^+\), \(\text{FAD}\) is used, and the enzyme is the membrane-bound Complex II that delivers electrons straight into the electron transport chain.
(Choice A) is incorrect: carriers are not relabeled. \(\text{NADH}\) and \(\text{FADH}_2\) are chemically distinct molecules; this step genuinely reduces \(\text{FAD}\), it does not convert an \(\text{NADH}\) into an \(\text{FADH}_2\).
(Choice B) is incorrect: it inverts the thermodynamics. The couple's potential is too high (near \(0\ \text{V}\), not negative enough), not too negative, and the enzyme is membrane-bound rather than free in the matrix.
(Choice D) is incorrect: this step is a dehydrogenation, not a decarboxylation, and no rule ties decarboxylations to \(\text{FAD}\); in fact the cycle's two decarboxylations both reduce \(\text{NAD}^+\), and the enzyme is membrane-embedded, not soluble.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, the student must reason from the reduction-potential ordering to justify the carrier and connect the step to its membrane location.$px$ WHERE id = 'bc03cc5a-d9bc-4f72-89db-c475c7dcecd5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Citric Acid Cycle.'

The answer is B because the cycle depends on the electron transport chain to reoxidize NADH and FADH2 back to \(\text{NAD}^+\) and FAD. Four of the cycle's steps are oxidations that need an oxidized carrier as the electron acceptor: isocitrate dehydrogenase, \(\alpha\)-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and malate dehydrogenase each reduce \(\text{NAD}^+\), while succinate dehydrogenase reduces FAD. When the chain is blocked, these reduced carriers pile up and the oxidized forms disappear; without \(\text{NAD}^+\) and FAD as electron acceptors, the dehydrogenation steps cannot proceed, so the cycle stops even though no enzyme uses \(\text{O}_2\) directly. The dependence is indirect, through carrier regeneration.

(Choice B) is correct: it identifies the coupling — a blocked chain fails to regenerate oxidized carriers, and the dehydrogenases stall for lack of \(\text{NAD}^+\)/FAD.

(Choice A) is incorrect: no cycle enzyme, including citrate synthase, binds \(\text{O}_2\); citrate synthase condenses acetyl-CoA with oxaloacetate. This choice contradicts the stem's own premise and invents a direct oxygen role that does not exist.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses the regulatory logic. Rising ADP signals low energy charge and activates isocitrate dehydrogenase; it does not inhibit it. If anything, a blocked chain that raises ADP would speed the cycle, not stop it. The actual shutdown here is a redox-cofactor problem, not ADP-mediated inhibition.

(Choice D) is incorrect: the decarboxylations are effectively irreversible oxidative steps and are not reversed by \(\text{CO}_2\) leakage or membrane potential changes; the actual failure is depletion of oxidized electron carriers.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, the student reasons from the cycle's coupling to the electron transport chain rather than from a memorized fact that the cycle is aerobic.$px$ WHERE id = 'bc2ec0ac-9954-489c-8955-6efeac922eb7';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO).' Because meiosis I separates homologous chromosomes, a failure to separate at anaphase I sends both members of the pair into one secondary spermatocyte and leaves the other secondary spermatocyte with neither, so both meiosis I products already carry an abnormal number of that chromosome before meiosis II begins. Each secondary spermatocyte then divides normally in meiosis II, faithfully copying its (abnormal) chromosome content into two sperm. When homologous chromosomes fail to separate during meiosis I, the outcome is two gametes with two copies and two gametes lacking that chromosome. (Choice A) is correct: the one spermatocyte yields four sperm, and because the error occurred before the cell split into two secondary spermatocytes, every one of the four is abnormal — two receive an extra copy (n+1) and two receive none (n−1). (Choice B) is incorrect: a two-normal, two-abnormal outcome results from nondisjunction in meiosis II, where only one secondary spermatocyte's sister chromatids fail to separate while the other divides correctly; here the failure is in meiosis I, so no normal sperm are produced. (Choice C) is incorrect: although both homologs do enter a single secondary spermatocyte, that cell still divides in meiosis II to produce two n+1 sperm, and the empty secondary spermatocyte produces two n−1 sperm, so far more than one sperm is affected. (Choice D) is incorrect: an even split between gain and loss is required because the two homologs and their absence are partitioned symmetrically into the two secondary spermatocytes, giving 2 and 2 rather than 3 and 1. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the timing of homolog separation in meiosis I to trace how a single mis-segregation propagates through both subsequent divisions and predict the final ratio of abnormal gametes.$px$ WHERE id = 'bcab131f-fcca-436a-81ee-4886fee27acd';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is C because blocking FSH receptors removes the Sertoli-cell signal that drives spermatogenesis, while LH continues to stimulate the Leydig cells, so testosterone output is unaffected. The text states that in the testes "FSH binds predominantly to the Sertoli cells within the seminiferous tubules to promote spermatogenesis" and, separately, that "LH binds to receptors on Leydig cells in the testes and upregulates the production of testosterone." Because the two gonadotropins act on different cell populations through different receptors, an antibody that blocks only FSH receptors silences the Sertoli (spermatogenic) arm while leaving the LH–Leydig (testosterone) arm intact. (Choice A) is wrong because Leydig-cell testosterone is driven by LH, not FSH, so testosterone production does not require FSH. (Choice B) reverses the cellular targets: FSH acts on Sertoli cells, not Leydig cells, so blocking FSH does not lower LH-driven testosterone. (Choice C) is correct because the Sertoli (FSH) arm fails while the Leydig (LH) arm keeps secreting testosterone. (Choice D) is wrong because FSH stimulates Sertoli cells to produce inhibins, so removing FSH signaling lowers inhibin rather than raising it. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the selective experimental outcome of a targeted receptor blockade by separating which gonadotropin acts on which testicular cell type.$px$ WHERE id = 'bcbc8141-5eb7-4f00-9d56-76e85d9e9af3';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is C because the released \(\text{PP}_i\) is a product of the activation reaction, and a ubiquitous inorganic pyrophosphatase cleaves it into two \(\text{P}_i\). By continuously removing \(\text{PP}_i\), the cell prevents the reverse reaction and, by Le Chatelier's principle, pulls the equilibrium toward acyl-CoA. This coupled hydrolysis is what converts a near-reversible step into an effectively irreversible one.

(Choice C) is correct: destroying the \(\text{PP}_i\) product removes it from the equilibrium, so the forward reaction is driven to completion.
(Choice A) is incorrect: rephosphorylating AMP to ATP would regenerate a reactant and, if anything, favor the reverse direction — the opposite of what makes activation irreversible.
(Choice B) is incorrect: the acyl-CoA thioester is indeed high-energy, but that reservoir of energy is what powers later steps; it does not cause the product to revert spontaneously.
(Choice D) is incorrect: the CPT-I transesterification moves the acyl group forward toward oxidation; it does not reverse the synthetase step, and it is not the driving force for irreversibility.

This item rewards Skill 2 reasoning: you must apply the principle of product removal (Le Chatelier) to explain how \(\text{PP}_i\) hydrolysis commits an otherwise reversible reaction.$px$ WHERE id = 'bd81137f-f2b6-4fe6-b3f9-fcca59db67b4';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is B because two unaffected parents who produce affected children indicate a recessive trait, and an affected daughter forces that recessive allele onto an autosome rather than the X chromosome. For a recessive disorder, affected individuals have the genotype \(aa\) while unaffected individuals are \(AA\) or \(Aa\); if neither parent is affected but a child is, both parents must be heterozygous carriers \(Aa\), and an \(Aa \times Aa\) cross yields affected \(aa\) offspring of either sex. The deciding clue is the affected daughter: for an X-linked recessive trait, a female must inherit the recessive X-linked allele from both parents in order to express it, which would require an affected (hemizygous \(X^a Y\)) father, but Parent 2 is unaffected — so X-linked recessive is impossible here, leaving autosomal recessive. (Choice A) is incorrect because a dominant trait cannot skip the parents: an affected child carrying the dominant allele would have to inherit it from an affected parent, yet both parents are unaffected. (Choice B) is correct: two unaffected carrier parents \(Aa \times Aa\) on an autosome can produce affected \(aa\) sons and daughters in equal likelihood, matching the pedigree. (Choice C) is incorrect because under X-linked recessive inheritance an affected daughter (\(X^a X^a\)) requires a father who is \(X^a Y\) and therefore affected; the unaffected father rules this out, and such disorders are disproportionately observed in males rather than split evenly across affected sons and daughters. (Choice D) is incorrect because, like any dominant pattern, an affected child must inherit the dominant allele from an affected parent, but neither parent is affected. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rules of recessive and X-linked inheritance to a pedigree and eliminate every mode that contradicts the observed affected son and daughter.$px$ WHERE id = 'bdcdc8ae-b224-4825-8caf-54ae717cb798';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is D because T4 (thyroxine) is the predominant secreted, longer-acting form that functions as a circulating prohormone reservoir, while peripheral tissues remove one iodine to convert it into the more active, faster-acting T3 — which is exactly why most of the active T3 is generated outside the gland and why its effects turn on and off more quickly than those of the longer-lived T4.

(Choice D) is correct: it captures the prohormone-reservoir model — T4 is the predominant secreted, long-acting form, and deiodination in peripheral tissues (liver, kidney, and others) yields the more potent, faster-acting T3, so most active T3 arises outside the gland and its effects come on and fade quickly.
(Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses which form is active — T3, not T4, is the more potent hormone at the receptor; T4 is the less-active prohormone, and T3 is the product of an activating conversion rather than an inactive degradation fragment.
(Choice B) is incorrect: the T4-to-T3 conversion occurs mainly in peripheral tissues via deiodinase enzymes, not inside the gland before secretion; the gland secretes mostly T4 while target tissues make their own T3, contradicting the claim that peripheral tissues cannot generate active hormone.
(Choice C) is incorrect: T4 and T3 are not equally potent, and T4 is in fact converted to T3 peripherally; treating them as interchangeable and denying any conversion ignores the reservoir/prohormone relationship that lets a steady pool of T4 supply active T3 on demand.

This question tests Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving) by asking you to integrate several observations — that the gland secretes mostly T4, that most active T3 is produced peripherally, and that T3 acts faster — into the prohormone-to-active-hormone relationship between T4 and T3.$px$ WHERE id = 'bde887d1-6409-41c6-a9b9-8f199caeac43';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC).' In a concentration cell both half-cells use the same electrode and the same redox couple, so the two standard reduction potentials are identical and \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = 0\ \text{V}\); the entire measured potential arises from the concentration difference and must be found with the Nernst equation. The cell runs spontaneously in the direction that equalizes the two concentrations: \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) is reduced in the concentrated half-cell (the cathode, where \([\text{Cu}^{2+}]\) falls) and \(\text{Cu}\) is oxidized in the dilute half-cell (the anode, where \([\text{Cu}^{2+}]\) rises). The reaction quotient is \(Q = \frac{[\text{Cu}^{2+}]_{\text{anode}}}{[\text{Cu}^{2+}]_{\text{cathode}}} = \frac{1.0\times10^{-3}}{1.0} = 1.0\times10^{-3}\), with \(n = 2\). (Choice B) is correct: \(E_{\text{cell}} = 0 - \frac{0.0592}{2}\log(1.0\times10^{-3}) = -0.0296(-3) = +0.089\ \text{V}\), a small positive value consistent with the spontaneous concentration-driven process. (Choice A) is incorrect: it assumes that because both electrodes are copper with identical \(E^{\circ}\), the cell potential must be zero; this ignores that a difference in ion concentration creates a real, nonzero potential through the \(\log Q\) term of the Nernst equation. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(-0.089\ \text{V}\) results from inverting the reaction quotient (placing the concentrated half-cell over the dilute one) or assigning the concentrated half-cell as the anode; a concentration cell is always spontaneous as written, so a negative potential signals a reversed assignment. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(+0.18\ \text{V}\) comes from using \(n = 1\) instead of \(n = 2\) in the Nernst prefactor (\(\frac{0.0592}{1}\times 3\)), the wrong electron count for the \(\text{Cu}^{2+}/\text{Cu}\) couple. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the Nernst equation to a concentration cell—recognizing \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = 0\), assigning cathode and anode from the concentrations, constructing \(Q\), and using \(n = 2\)—rather than reading a value from a table.$px$ WHERE id = 'bf7d8e7d-0f7d-4f4c-abee-739a12428e1d';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is C because the data describe rising oxygen consumption with little extra ATP and heightened ion pumping—the signature of thyroid hormone speeding up Na+/K+-ATPase turnover (which continuously burns ATP to maintain gradients) while also causing mild mitochondrial uncoupling, so oxidized substrate is dissipated as heat rather than banked as ATP.

(Choice C) is correct: raising Na+/K+-ATPase turnover raises the ATP-consuming workload of the resting cell, and mild uncoupling lets oxidation proceed while releasing energy as heat, matching the observed high \(O_2\) use, low ATP gain, and increased ion pumping.
(Choice A) is incorrect: tighter coupling would channel oxidation into ATP and produce little heat, the opposite of the low-ATP, high-heat pattern described.
(Choice B) is incorrect: the cells show increased, not decreased, ion-pumping activity, so suppressing Na+/K+-ATPase cannot explain the higher resting oxygen demand.
(Choice D) is incorrect: oxygen consumption rises rather than being blocked, so the cell is not shifting toward anaerobic glycolysis; thyroid hormone drives aerobic oxidation and heat output.

This item tests Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving (Skill 2): you must infer the underlying mechanism from a described pattern of oxygen use, ATP yield, and ion-pump activity rather than recall that thyroid hormone raises BMR.$px$ WHERE id = 'bfdeb6c2-0f4b-420c-ab6e-093193857579';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Mechanisms of Hormone Action.' The answer is B because cortisol exerts negative feedback on the anterior pituitary's secretion of ACTH; when the adrenal cortex itself fails (primary insufficiency), cortisol is low and can no longer suppress the pituitary, so ACTH rises markedly, whereas if the pituitary is the failing organ (secondary insufficiency) it cannot make ACTH, so ACTH is inappropriately low. Measuring ACTH therefore separates the two: a high ACTH with low cortisol localizes the defect to the adrenal gland. (Choice B) is correct: a high plasma ACTH with low cortisol reflects intact pituitary drive that has lost its cortisol-mediated negative feedback, meaning the adrenal cortex cannot respond — the hallmark of primary adrenal failure. (Choice A) is incorrect: repeating the cortisol measurement confirms only that cortisol is low; it gives no information about which organ in the axis is responsible, because low cortisol occurs in both primary and secondary insufficiency. (Choice C) is incorrect: a low, suppressed ACTH with low cortisol indicates the pituitary is not providing tropic drive, which localizes the defect to the pituitary (secondary), not to the adrenal cortex. (Choice D) is incorrect: CRH is released into the hypophyseal portal circulation and is not reliably measurable in peripheral blood, and even conceptually a low CRH would implicate the hypothalamus/pituitary axis rather than the adrenal gland. This item exercises the skill of using a tropic hormone level and the direction of negative feedback to localize a lesion within an endocrine axis.$px$ WHERE id = 'bff43e8c-228f-4965-89ca-065896048515';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$The Fischer projection shown depicts the open-chain form of a common aldohexose, drawn with the aldehyde carbon at the top and the terminal CH2OH at the bottom. A chiral molecule and its enantiomer are non-superimposable mirror images of one another. If the sugar in the structure shown were replaced by its enantiomer and that enantiomer were drawn as a Fischer projection in the same top-to-bottom orientation, which set of hydroxyl (–OH) positions — listed for the four stereocenters in order from the top stereocenter down to the bottom stereocenter — would correctly represent it?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is D because the enantiomer of a sugar is its non-superimposable mirror image, and generating that mirror image in a Fischer projection requires reflecting the horizontal bonds at every stereocenter — so each –OH that points one way in the original must point the opposite way in the enantiomer. Reading the four stereocenters of the structure shown from top to bottom gives the sequence right, left, right, right; reflecting all four yields left, right, left, left, which is the enantiomer (the L form).

(Choice D) is correct: every one of the four stereocenters is inverted relative to the structure shown. Because all chiral centers are simultaneously flipped, the result is the true mirror image (enantiomer), consistent with the fact that the mirror image of D-glucose is L-glucose.

(Choice B) is incorrect: this set inverts only the bottom (penultimate) stereocenter and leaves the other three unchanged. Inverting the single carbon most remote from the carbonyl swaps the D/L designation, but because the other three centers are untouched the molecule is not the mirror image — it is a diastereomer that differs at just one center (an epimer), not the enantiomer.

(Choice C) is incorrect: this set reproduces the structure shown exactly, with no stereocenter inverted. A molecule is superimposable on itself, so this is simply the original sugar again, not its mirror image.

(Choice A) is incorrect: this set inverts only the top stereocenter and leaves the lower three unchanged. Flipping a single center produces a diastereomer that differs at one position, not the fully reflected mirror image required for an enantiomer.

This question requires Skill 2 reasoning because the diagnostic stereochemical pattern must be extracted from the figure and then transformed by applying the definition of an enantiomer, distinguishing a full four-center reflection from partial inversions that yield diastereomers.$px$ WHERE id = 'c0183456-928f-4fd4-a0b2-c3ccc5170a9a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is A because the vessels that empty into the left atrium are the pulmonary veins, and the blood they carry has just traversed the alveolar capillary beds, where it equilibrated with alveolar air and became oxygenated — giving it a high partial pressure of oxygen relative to the deoxygenated blood in the right ventricle. (Choice A) is correct: blood returning to the left atrium arrives via the pulmonary veins after being oxygenated in the lungs, so its high PO2 versus the right-ventricular sample is exactly what is expected. (Choice B) is incorrect: it names the correct 'artery-carries-away-from-heart' generality but misapplies it — the pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood to the lungs and does not empty into the left atrium, so it cannot be the high-PO2 atrial sample. (Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly identifies a pulmonary vein but gives a false reason — pulmonary veins have not delivered oxygen to systemic tissues; systemic oxygen delivery occurs in the systemic capillaries, and the pulmonary veins instead carry newly oxygenated blood from the lungs. (Choice D) is incorrect: the pulmonary artery leaves the right ventricle carrying deoxygenated blood toward the lungs — it has not yet been oxygenated — so it would be low, not high, in PO2 and does not feed the left atrium. Skill: This item requires applying knowledge of pulmonary circulation routing to reason about the oxygen state of blood in a named vessel rather than recalling a definition.$px$ WHERE id = 'c0545a4a-0e26-43b9-bb93-5652958306f0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is C because a zymogen (proenzyme) is an inactive precursor that becomes active only after a covalent biochemical change to its polypeptide: a zymogen, also called a proenzyme, is an inactive precursor of an enzyme that requires a biochemical change—such as a hydrolysis reaction that reveals the active site or a configurational change that exposes it—to become an active enzyme. For trypsinogen specifically, enteropeptidase activates the zymogen by removing the 7-10 amino acid trypsinogen activation peptide (TAP) from the N-terminal region, and removal of TAP induces a conformational change that results in active trypsin. More generally, zymogen activation through specific proteolytic cleavage or conformational change ensures that enzymes such as digestive proteases or caspases become active only under appropriate conditions; the analogous chymotrypsinogen loses its first 15 amino acids by selective proteolysis upon activation. (Choice A) is incorrect: phosphorylation is a post-translational modification, one of the reversible PTMs (alongside acetylation, glycosylation, and ubiquitination); it is a distinct layer of regulation from zymogen activation and does not describe the irreversible proteolytic maturation of a proenzyme. (Choice B) is incorrect: allosteric regulation involves binding events at sites distant from an enzyme's active site that induce conformational changes to modulate activity; this reversible ligand binding is a separate regulatory mechanism and does not cleave the inactive precursor. (Choice C) is correct: activation of a zymogen occurs by proteolytic cleavage that irreversibly removes part of the precursor polypeptide, as when enteropeptidase excises the trypsinogen activation peptide to yield active trypsin. (Choice D) is incorrect: activation requires an enzymatic biochemical change (such as a hydrolysis reaction revealing the active site) carried out by a protease such as enteropeptidase; a mere change in pH does not cleave the polypeptide and is not the described activation mechanism. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that zymogens are inactive proenzymes activated by irreversible proteolytic cleavage rather than by reversible modifications such as phosphorylation, allosteric binding, or pH change.$px$ WHERE id = 'c0fdece0-ac92-42e9-8c76-bf35e9dcc500';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' Smooth muscle is organized in two functional forms: single-unit (visceral) smooth muscle, whose fibers are joined by gap junctions so that the whole sheet contracts as a single unit and which displays a stress-relaxation (stretch-triggered) response, and multiunit smooth muscle, whose cells rarely possess gap junctions, are not electrically coupled, and are driven only by autonomic nerves or hormones. The two behaviors described in the strip—a contraction that spreads cell-to-cell as a coordinated wave, and a contraction that can be started by stretch alone—are the defining functional signatures of single-unit visceral muscle, and both trace to one structural feature. (Choice A) is incorrect: dedicated one-to-one autonomic innervation of each fiber is characteristic of multiunit smooth muscle, in which contraction stays confined to the stimulated cell and cannot be triggered by stretch, so it explains neither the spreading wave nor the stretch response. (Choice B) is correct: gap junctions electrically couple neighboring fibers, letting depolarization pass directly from cell to cell so a stimulus applied to one cell produces a propagating wave, and this same coupled, single-unit organization underlies the stress-relaxation response by which stretch triggers contraction. (Choice C) is incorrect: intercalated discs join cardiac muscle cells, not smooth muscle fibers; although they do contain gap junctions, they are the wrong structure for this tissue, and citing them misattributes a cardiac feature to smooth muscle. (Choice D) is incorrect: smooth muscle fibers are small in diameter and lack a T-tubule system entirely, relying instead on sarcolemmal calcium channels; T-tubules therefore cannot explain the observed spread of contraction between cells. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between single-unit and multiunit smooth muscle to interpret two experimental observations and infer the coupling structure responsible for both.$px$ WHERE id = 'c12d925a-50b0-44bc-aace-d6f6d2807bfa';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A crystallographer characterizes two β-sheet domains, X and Y, that have the same number of strands, the same strand length, and nearly identical amino acid composition. In domain X, each inter-strand backbone hydrogen bond is essentially colinear: the donor amide N–H points almost straight at the acceptor carbonyl O (deviation < 5°). In domain Y, the same backbone hydrogen bonds are noticeably skewed, deviating roughly 20–25° from linear. When the two domains are thermally denatured under identical conditions, domain X unfolds at a distinctly higher melting temperature than domain Y. Which conclusion about the strand arrangement in the two domains is BEST supported by these observations?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' In a β-pleated sheet the strands are held together by inter-strand backbone hydrogen bonds between amide H and carbonyl O atoms, but the geometry of those bonds depends on strand direction: when adjacent strands run in opposite directions, the carbonyl O sits exactly opposite the amide H so the hydrogen bonds are straight (near-linear), whereas when strands run in the same direction the carbonyl O is not directly opposite the amide H, so the hydrogen bonds are bent significantly. Because straighter hydrogen bonds are stronger, the opposite-direction arrangement is the more stable and higher-melting one, which is why short same-direction sheets are comparatively uncommon. (Choice A) is correct: domain X has the near-linear hydrogen bonds and higher melting temperature that are the signature of the more stable, opposite-direction (antiparallel) sheet, while the bent bonds and lower stability of domain Y match the same-direction (parallel) arrangement. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the relationship—it is the antiparallel (opposite-direction) sheet, not the parallel one, whose geometry allows the straight, stronger hydrogen bonds, so the labels are swapped. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(i \rightarrow i+4\) hydrogen bonds are the intra-strand bonds of an α-helix, not the inter-strand bonds of a β-sheet, so invoking them to explain a difference between two sheets misapplies helix geometry to sheet stability. (Choice D) is incorrect: the data attribute the stability difference to hydrogen-bond geometry (linear vs. bent) in otherwise matched domains, and nothing indicates cysteine content or disulfide cross-links, so adding disulfides is an unsupported explanation. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the link between β-sheet hydrogen-bond geometry, strand orientation, and relative stability to interpret structural and thermal data and infer the strand arrangement.$px$ WHERE id = 'c1c05fb2-4b38-4bce-b2e4-71df5b9c6308';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is B because a transporter carries substrate in proportion to its concentration only while it is well below saturation. GLUT2 has a Km well above normal blood glucose (roughly \( 15\text{--}20\,\text{mM} \) versus a plasma glucose near \( 5\,\text{mM} \)), so across the physiological range it never approaches \( V_{max} \). Glucose entry therefore rises nearly linearly with plasma glucose, glycolytic ATP production rises with it, and the resulting ATP signal (closing K_ATP channels, depolarizing the cell, and admitting Ca\(^{2+}\)) scales insulin secretion to the prevailing glucose level.

(Choice B) is correct: staying far from saturation is exactly what lets uptake — and the downstream ATP trigger — increase in step with blood glucose, matching secretion to the glucose level.
(Choice A) is incorrect: it inverts the meaning of a high Km. A high Km means the transporter saturates only at high glucose, so at physiological levels it is not maximal, uptake is not constant, and secretion is graded rather than fixed.
(Choice C) is incorrect: GLUT2 is insulin-independent, not insulin-dependent; the beta cell senses glucose directly, without needing insulin to recruit its transporter (that recruitment describes GLUT4 in muscle and fat).
(Choice D) is incorrect: its conclusion is right but its mechanism is wrong — GLUT transporters move glucose by facilitated diffusion down its gradient, not by active transport against a gradient.

This question tests Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving) because it asks you to reason from a transporter's kinetic property (high Km) to the physiological behavior it produces (graded, glucose-proportional secretion).$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"Because a high-Km transporter saturates at low glucose, uptake is already maximal at every physiological level, so the beta cell secretes a fixed amount of insulin no matter the glucose.","label":"A"},{"text":"Because GLUT2's Km sits well above normal blood glucose, the transporter stays far from saturation, so glucose entry — and the ATP signal it generates — rises almost in step with plasma glucose.","label":"B"},{"text":"Because GLUT2 is insulin-dependent, rising insulin recruits more GLUT2 to the membrane, so a feed-forward loop between insulin and glucose entry sets the beta cell's secretion rate.","label":"C"},{"text":"Because GLUT2 actively pumps glucose against its gradient at a rate set by blood glucose, it directly drives insulin secretion in proportion to the prevailing blood level.","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'c2711a6f-79c9-42ec-bb9e-d33e2e088857';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth.' During gestation the placenta takes over the nutrient and waste requirements of the fetus: the parent's blood delivers nutrients to the placenta and removes waste from it, and chemicals from the fetus, such as bilirubin, are processed by the parent's liver for elimination. A fetus whose own liver cannot clear a waste product can still remain healthy because the maternal circulation handles that clearance across the placenta; the burden shifts to the newborn only once the placental connection is severed at birth. (Choice A) is correct: because the placenta let the parent's blood carry fetal waste (including bilirubin) to the parent's liver for processing, the fetus's own enzyme deficiency was masked, and the waste only accumulated once that maternal clearance was lost at birth. (Choice B) is incorrect: the fetal liver is active during gestation (it is one of the first organs to form and functions in the fetus), so bilirubin is indeed produced before birth — it is simply cleared by the parent, not by the fetal enzyme. (Choice C) is incorrect: maternal immunoglobulins do cross the placenta, but they provide passive immunity against infections and do not chemically neutralize a metabolic waste product like bilirubin. (Choice D) is incorrect: fetal red blood cells are continually broken down and replaced during gestation, so bilirubin is generated in the fetus; the reason it does not accumulate is placental clearance, not an absence of red-cell turnover. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that the placenta shifts fetal waste handling to the parent's circulation to deduce why an inherited clearance defect stays silent until birth.$px$ WHERE id = 'c29e68c1-7aae-4dcb-af67-dc5d17ac50f0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO)'. The answer is D because the prostate is the gland responsible for the alkaline secretion that first coagulates and then decoagulates semen, and this clot-then-liquefy step is what retains semen in the female reproductive tract. The text states the prostate "excretes an alkaline, milky fluid... that is critical to first coagulate and then decoagulate the semen following ejaculation. The temporary thickening of semen helps retain it within the female reproductive tract, providing time for sperm to utilize the fructose provided by seminal vesicle secretions." Removing the prostate eliminates this coagulation–liquefaction cycle, so semen is poorly retained during the very interval when sperm rely on seminal-vesicle fructose for motility; because the seminal vesicles remain intact, the fructose itself is still supplied, making the retention defect the key consequence. (Choice A) is wrong because the fructose used by sperm mitochondria comes from the seminal vesicles, not the prostate, and the seminal vesicles were left intact. (Choice B) is a partial truth: the prostate does secrete alkaline fluid, but the seminal vesicles also contribute alkaline fluid, so the prostate is not the sole buffer, and the text emphasizes the prostate's coagulation role rather than buffering as the unique critical function. (Choice C) misassigns the epididymis's role, since it is in the epididymis that sperm "further mature and acquire the ability to move under their own power," not the prostate. (Choice D) is correct because removing the prostate disrupts the coagulate-then-decoagulate retention step while seminal-vesicle fructose remains available. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the downstream physiological consequence of removing one accessory gland by chaining the prostate's coagulation function to the seminal vesicles' separate fructose contribution and reasoning about how the loss alters semen retention.$px$ WHERE id = 'c2ae4b7f-e799-4652-b027-b61de85711cb';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is B because the \(\text{pI}\) is found by averaging the \(\text{pK}_a\) values of the two groups closest to and on either side of the net-neutral species. Arginine is basic, so as pH rises the \(\alpha\text{-}\text{COOH}\) (\(\text{pK}_a\) \(2.0\)) loses its proton first, taking the molecule from \(+2\) to a still-positive \(+1\); the molecule only reaches net zero after the \(\alpha\text{-}\text{NH}_3^+\) (\(\text{pK}_a\) \(9.0\)) deprotonates, and the very last group to leave is the guanidinium side chain (\(\text{pK}_a\) \(12.5\)). The net-neutral zwitterion is therefore flanked by the two HIGHEST \(\text{pK}_a\) values, so \(\text{pI} = (9.0 + 12.5)/2 = 10.75\). (Choice A) \(5.5\) averages the two LOWEST \(\text{pK}_a\) (\((2.0 + 9.0)/2\)) — that is the formula for a neutral amino acid, but arginine's basic side chain means the neutral form sits higher, between \(9.0\) and \(12.5\). (Choice B) is correct: averaging the two \(\text{pK}_a\) flanking the net-neutral form, \((9.0 + 12.5)/2 = 10.75\). (Choice C) \(7.25\) averages the lowest and highest \(\text{pK}_a\) (\((2.0 + 12.5)/2\)), skipping the middle group, so it does not bracket the neutral species. (Choice D) \(7.8\) averages all three \(\text{pK}_a\) (\((2.0 + 9.0 + 12.5)/3\)) instead of only the two that flank net neutrality. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that \(\text{pI}\) is the average of the two \(\text{pK}_a\) bracketing the net-neutral form to a specific basic amino acid and select the correct pair.$px$ WHERE id = 'c2bfc7cf-4ad1-4f70-b012-4353059dcd43';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separations and Purifications.' The answer is A because migration in a gel depends on charge density and shape, not mass alone; on a native gel each protein keeps its own net charge and folded shape, so position cannot be read as molecular weight (exactly what the data show). Coating every protein with an anionic detergent such as SDS binds in constant proportion to mass (~1.4 g per gram of protein) and masks the intrinsic charges, giving every species the same charge density and a uniform rod-like shape. Mobility then depends only on sieving through the gel, so migration becomes a function of molecular weight alone and mass can be read against the standards.

(Choice A) is correct: normalizing charge density with an anionic detergent removes charge and shape as variables, leaving mass as the sole determinant of migration — the requirement for reading mass off a standard curve.

(Choice B) is incorrect: raising the voltage speeds every protein up but changes nothing about the relationship between mobility and mass; a native protein's migration still reflects its charge density and shape, so the standards still fail to predict mass.

(Choice C) is incorrect: isoelectric focusing separates proteins by isoelectric point (pI) along a pH gradient, not by size, so it reports the pH at which a protein is neutral — not its molecular mass.

(Choice D) is incorrect: a reducing agent only cleaves disulfide bonds; without a detergent to normalize charge density, the released chains still migrate according to their individual charge and shape, so mass still cannot be read from the standards.

This question requires the Skill 2 ability to reason with a separations concept: recognizing from the failed native-gel data that charge and shape confound size, and selecting the technique that normalizes charge density so that migration reports molecular mass.$px$ WHERE id = 'c335c4c7-6809-4801-ae87-8741587e1b30';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is C because all three signals point the same direction. Low insulin removes the fed-state brake on hormone-sensitive lipase (HSL), while epinephrine and cortisol are stress/fasting hormones that add positive drive onto HSL. When a suppressor is withdrawn and two activators are added, the net effect is strong stimulation of lipolysis and release of fatty acids from stored triacylglycerol.

(Choice C) is correct: the signals converge — falling insulin disinhibits HSL and epinephrine and cortisol stimulate it, so their effects sum to favor mobilization of stored fat.
(Choice A) is incorrect: it assigns epinephrine and cortisol an inhibitory role. These are lipolytic stress hormones that activate HSL; they reinforce, not oppose, the effect of low insulin.
(Choice B) is incorrect: HSL is not controlled by insulin alone. Epinephrine and cortisol independently activate HSL, which is precisely why stress and fasting both mobilize fat.
(Choice D) is incorrect: it confuses the enzymes. Fatty-acid storage via lipoprotein lipase is favored by insulin in the fed state; low insulin plus stress hormones drives release, not storage.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, it asks the student to integrate several hormonal inputs onto a single regulated enzyme rather than recall one hormone in isolation.$px$ WHERE id = 'c34afa80-036b-4483-8540-e2fd3fa19cd0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is A because, during the mid-follicular phase, high plasma estrogen from the growing follicles drives a classic negative-feedback loop that suppresses gonadotropin output, lowering FSH and starving most follicles of the support they need to survive. The text states that "high concentrations of estrogen will stimulate the hypothalamus and pituitary to reduce the production of GnRH, LH, and FSH," and that "because the large tertiary follicles require FSH to grow and survive at this point, this decline in FSH caused by negative feedback leads most of them to die (atresia)." Only one follicle, the dominant follicle, typically survives this drop in FSH. The switch to positive feedback occurs only later, when the lone dominant follicle produces such extreme estrogen levels that normal negative feedback fails. (Choice A) is correct: estrogen's negative feedback at the hypothalamus and pituitary lowers FSH, and the FSH decline causes atresia of most follicles. (Choice B) reverses the feedback direction; the positive feedback loop "only occurs at this point in the cycle"—near ovulation with the dominant follicle—not in the mid-follicular phase. (Choice C) is a partial truth because progesterone does suppress gonadotropins, but estrogen also exerts negative feedback during the follicular phase, so FSH does fall. (Choice D) misplaces the feedback target; estrogen acts on the hypothalamus and pituitary to reduce gonadotropin output, not directly on the ovarian follicles. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the direction of a hormonal feedback response from a described physiological scenario and tracing that change to its downstream effect on follicle survival.$px$ WHERE id = 'c3938d1a-0c8d-4713-8cde-7fb05b266813';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Impulse Propagation & Synapses'. The answer is C because the action potential is an all-or-none event: once threshold is crossed, every action potential a given neuron fires runs the identical course and peaks at the same voltage, so a stronger stimulus cannot make a single spike any taller. The text states that "All action potentials peak at the same voltage (+30 mV), so one action potential is not bigger than another," and that "Stronger stimuli will initiate multiple action potentials more quickly, but the individual signals are not bigger." Stimulus intensity is therefore encoded by the frequency (and number) of action potentials, not by their amplitude. (Choice A) is wrong because a taller spike from extra Na+ influx is exactly the intensity-equals-amplitude error the all-or-none principle refutes; the peak is fixed at about +30 mV regardless of how far past threshold the stimulus drives the membrane. (Choice B) is wrong because action potential duration is set by the intrinsic kinetics of the voltage-gated channels (the inactivation gate "closes after a specific period of time—on the order of a fraction of a millisecond"), not by how strong the stimulus is. (Choice C) is correct because every action potential the neuron fires reaches the same peak voltage, so the stronger stimulus yields spikes of identical height. (Choice D) is wrong because threshold is a fixed membrane value ("Once the membrane reaches that voltage [-55 mV]... This is what is known as the threshold"); stimulus strength does not lower or reset that value. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the all-or-none principle—that action potential amplitude is fixed and independent of suprathreshold stimulus strength.$px$ WHERE id = 'c3a46aab-4292-4616-8c7c-cf01d9befe26';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is A because removing CO2 from the blood by rapid breathing lowers the CO2 concentration, which pulls the coupled equilibrium (CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 ⇌ H+ + HCO3−) to the left; this leftward shift consumes free H+, so hydrogen-ion concentration drops and, because pH is inversely related to [H+], blood pH rises (a respiratory alkalosis). (Choice A) is correct: exhaling CO2 faster than it is produced shifts the buffer toward CO2, consuming H+ and lowering [H+], which raises pH. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the direction of the shift — increased [H+] with lower pH describes what happens when CO2 accumulates (hypoventilation/breath-holding), not when it is blown off. (Choice C) is incorrect: it pairs the correct pH change with the wrong [H+] change; a rising pH must come from a falling [H+], so [H+] cannot increase here. (Choice D) is incorrect: it pairs the correct [H+] change with the wrong pH direction; because pH varies inversely with [H+], a decrease in [H+] cannot lower pH. This question tests the skill of reasoning about the direction a coupled buffer equilibrium shifts when a reactant (CO2) is removed.$px$ WHERE id = 'c3aa9dfa-2736-4c7c-99bc-0f940ca0c8f3';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Impulse Propagation & Synapses'. The answer is D because axon X combines the two geometric features that both lower resistance and speed conduction: a wide diameter and a short length. Diameter functions as a resistance factor: the wave of Na+-based depolarization travels faster along a wide axon than along a narrow one, and overall conduction speed reflects both the axon's diameter and its resistance to current leak. A wide diameter lowers internal resistance, and a shorter path adds less resistance, so the wide-and-short axon (X) is faster than the wide-and-long axon (W), which fights extra length-resistance. Because diameter dominates, both wide axons beat both narrow axons, and among the wide pair the shorter one wins outright. (Choice A) Axon W is wide, so it beats the narrow axons, but it is also the longest, adding length-resistance that the equally wide but shorter axon X avoids, so W is not the single fastest. (Choice B) Axon Y is the narrowest, which raises internal resistance and slows the wave—the opposite of the wide-river effect—so it is among the slowest, not the fastest. (Choice C) Axon Z is shortest, which helps, but it is narrow; since diameter outweighs length, its narrowness makes it slower than the wide axon X. (Choice D) Axon X is both wide (low resistance, the dominant factor) and short (least added resistance), so it is the fastest of the four. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires combining two competing geometric variables—diameter and length—and applying the rule that the diameter (resistance) effect dominates to predict which axon conducts fastest.$px$ WHERE id = 'c3f3c61b-2312-4662-944e-93f7a3dc2fd8';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because the flat, pre-tension interval of a twitch is the latent period, during which the action potential is being propagated along the sarcolemma and \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) ions are released from the SR while contraction has yet to occur. In this first phase, excitation and contraction are being coupled, but shortening has not yet begun. Tension stays at baseline precisely because cross-bridge cycling and sarcomere shortening have not yet begun; those events define the subsequent contraction phase, when tension rises. (Choice A) Cross-bridges cycling as sarcomeres actively shorten occurs in the contraction phase, when cross-bridges have formed and the sarcomeres shorten toward peak tension; this produces the tension rise, not the flat latent interval. (Choice B) Pumping \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) from the sarcoplasm back into the SR belongs to the relaxation phase, when \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) ions are returned to the SR and cross-bridge cycling stops, which is the opposite direction of \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) movement from the latent period's release. (Choice C) Tropomyosin shifting off actin as cross-bridges form is part of the contraction phase, when tropomyosin has moved away from the myosin-binding sites on actin and cross-bridges have formed; although \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release has occurred by the latent period, the filament-level steps of tropomyosin movement and cross-bridge formation have not yet happened. (Choice D) Correctly captures the latent period because excitation-contraction coupling—AP propagation plus SR \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release—is underway while no shortening has begun, keeping tension at baseline. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall which molecular events define the latent period of a twitch versus the contraction and relaxation phases.$px$ WHERE id = 'c437d42b-90a1-4dbb-abce-3d5755ea7b60';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' For a representative element, the period number equals the highest principal quantum number \(n\) of any occupied shell, not the order in which subshells were filled by the Aufbau principle. In \([\text{Ar}]4s^2 3d^{10} 4p^1\), the \(4s\) and \(4p\) electrons occupy the \(n=4\) shell, while the \(3d\) electrons—though written after \(4s\) because they are lower in energy—belong to the already-buried \(n=3\) shell; the largest occupied value of \(n\) is \(4\). (Choice B) is correct: the outermost occupied shell is \(n=4\) (holding the \(4s\) and \(4p\) valence electrons), so gallium sits in period 4, even though the last-written subshell is \(3d\). (Choice A) is incorrect: \(n=3\) is the shell of the filled \(3d^{10}\) subshell, but those \(d\) electrons count as core, not valence, and the atom still occupies the higher \(n=4\) shell, so the period is fixed by the maximum \(n\), not by whichever subshell was written last. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(n=5\) would require electrons in a fifth shell, but no \(5s\), \(5p\), or higher orbital is occupied here; counting the number of distinct subshells written (or adding one past \(n=4\)) overshoots the actual outermost shell. (Choice D) is incorrect: the \(10\) is the superscript giving the number of electrons in the \(3d\) subshell, not a principal quantum number; treating the largest digit in the configuration as the period confuses electron count with shell number. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that the period equals the highest occupied principal quantum number, distinguishing the outermost shell from the energy-based filling order and from raw electron counts.$px$ WHERE id = 'c439ade1-c1cc-4f17-b4aa-955a20787481';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is B because a (+)sense RNA genome functions as mRNA and is translated immediately, a (−)sense RNA genome must first be copied to a (+)sense strand by a virion-carried RNA-dependent RNA polymerase before any protein can be made, and a retrovirus (despite being (+)sense) does not translate first but instead reverse-transcribes its RNA into DNA using a virion-carried reverse transcriptase. The text states that "If a virus has a +ssRNA genome, it can be translated directly to make viral proteins. Viral genomic +ssRNA acts like cellular mRNA," whereas "if a virus contains a −ssRNA genome, the host ribosomes cannot translate it until the −ssRNA is replicated into +ssRNA by viral RNA-dependent RNA polymerase (RdRP)... The RdRP is brought in by the virus." For the retrovirus, the text specifies that "Single-stranded RNA viruses such as HIV carry a special enzyme called reverse transcriptase within the capsid that synthesizes a complementary ssDNA (cDNA) copy using the +ssRNA genome as a template." The polarity trap is that a retrovirus is itself (+)sense, so a test-taker who reasons only from polarity would predict immediate translation; the distinguishing feature is the virion-packaged enzyme that diverts the genome to DNA first. (Choice A) reverses the picornavirus and (−)sense behaviors, having the readable (+)sense genome copied and the unreadable (−)sense genome translated. (Choice B) is correct: each genome's first event matches its required machinery — direct translation, copy-then-translate, and reverse-transcribe-then-integrate. (Choice C) wrongly assigns reverse transcription to the picornavirus and demotes the retrovirus to mere RNA-to-RNA copying, ignoring its packaged reverse transcriptase. (Choice D) is the partial-truth trap: it correctly translates the (+)sense picornavirus but swaps the (−)sense and retrovirus events, attributing reverse transcriptase to the (−)sense virus (which actually carries RdRP) and RdRP-style copying to the retrovirus. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between genome polarity and required viral enzymes to predict, for three distinct genomes, which molecular event must occur first after uncoating.$px$ WHERE id = 'c475756d-787e-4799-a54e-1cf1734ffb59';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Mechanisms of Development.' A stem cell is defined by two properties working together: self-renewal (it can divide without limit and regenerate more cells identical to itself) and the potency to differentiate, under specific conditions, into one or more specialized cell types. Stem cells continually divide and regenerate new stem cells rather than further specializing, and hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow both persist and give rise to the various blood cell types. A correct lineage trace must therefore show BOTH continuous self-renewal AND ongoing production of specialized cells; a cell showing only one of these is a progenitor, a terminal cell, or a non-differentiating proliferator. (Choice A) is incorrect: a founder whose descendants all become one mature cell type and then stop dividing has differentiated and lost self-renewal—this is a terminally differentiated (or short-lived progenitor) lineage, not a stem cell. (Choice B) is correct: it uniquely captures both defining features—the founder keeps regenerating cells identical to itself (self-renewal, "divide without limit") while also giving rise to several specialized cell types (multilineage differentiation)—and does so indefinitely, which is exactly the behavior expected of a stem cell. (Choice C) is incorrect: unlimited division with no descendant ever specializing shows self-renewal but no differentiation potency, so the potency criterion of a stem cell is not met. (Choice D) is incorrect: producing multiple specialized cell types satisfies potency, but because the founder-like cells are exhausted and never regenerated, self-renewal fails—this describes a multipotent progenitor, not a stem cell. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the twin criteria of self-renewal and differentiation to interpret single-cell lineage-tracing data and select the outcome that satisfies both.$px$ WHERE id = 'c501f3e9-2fa4-4d58-97c9-10fba7032837';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because chemical equations must be balanced to honor the law of conservation of mass, which at the atomic level means atoms are neither created nor destroyed during a reaction — they are only rearranged. Consequently, every element must appear in equal numbers on the reactant and product sides. In \(\text{C}_3\text{H}_8 + 5\,\text{O}_2 \rightarrow 3\,\text{CO}_2 + 4\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\), the coefficients are chosen so that carbon (3 = 3), hydrogen (8 = 8), and oxygen (\(5\times2 = 10\) on the left; \(3\times2 + 4\times1 = 10\) on the right) each tally to the same count on both sides, which is exactly what conservation of atoms requires. (Choice A) Energy conservation is a real principle, but balancing coefficients track atom counts, not energy bookkeeping; combustion is exothermic and its energy is not made equal across the arrow. (Choice C) Equal charge on both sides is required only for net ionic equations involving ions; this equation contains neutral molecules, and charge balance is not what fixes the atom counts here. (Choice D) The number of molecules (or moles of molecules) is generally not conserved — here 6 reactant molecules become 7 product molecules — so molecule count is not the conserved quantity that drives balancing. Skill 1$px$ WHERE id = 'c57309a7-a786-48cb-911f-21673694b93f';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers'. The answer is D because the intestinal wall is a mixed-origin organ: its inner epithelial (mucosal) lining is endoderm-derived, whereas its smooth-muscle muscularis arises from mesoderm, so silencing an endoderm-only factor disrupts the lining but spares the muscle. The endoderm gives rise to the lining of the digestive tract, including the epithelium of the stomach, intestines, liver, and pancreas, while the mesoderm gives rise to specialized muscle tissues, including cardiac muscle and the smooth muscle of the intestines, so the two tissue layers trace to two different germ layers. The endoderm generates the lining tissues such as the mucosae of the digestive and respiratory systems, whereas the mesoderm gives rise to most of the muscle and connective tissues. Because the mutation is restricted to the endoderm, only the endoderm-derived mucosal epithelium is affected. (Choice A) is wrong because the whole gut wall is not endoderm; the muscularis is mesodermal, so it would not fail. (Choice B) is wrong because it reverses the assignment—muscle is mesodermal, not the endodermal contribution, so the muscularis would survive and the lining would fail, not the opposite. (Choice C) is wrong because vascular endothelium derives from mesoderm, not endoderm, so the vessels in the wall would be unaffected by an endoderm-only defect. (Choice D) is correct because the endoderm-derived mucosal lining fails while the mesoderm-derived muscularis develops normally. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires applying germ-layer origin rules to a novel mixed-origin organ and predicting which tissue layer a layer-specific mutation will disrupt.$px$ WHERE id = 'c5b62142-8a8d-4e74-be89-0022dae157a5';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A \(0.3150\ \text{g}\) sample of impure sodium oxalate, Na\(_2\)C\(_2\)O\(_4\) (molar mass \(134.00\ \text{g/mol}\); the only redox-active component), is dissolved in acid and treated with \(60.00\ \text{mL}\) of \(0.02000\ \text{M}\) KMnO\(_4\)—an excess—so that all of the oxalate is oxidized to CO\(_2\) while permanganate is reduced to Mn\(^{2+}\). The unreacted permanganate is then back-titrated with \(0.05000\ \text{M}\) Fe\(^{2+}\), reaching the disappearance of the purple color after \(30.00\ \text{mL}\); in this back-titration MnO\(_4^-\) and Fe\(^{2+}\) react in a \(1{:}5\) mole ratio, while permanganate and oxalate react in a \(2{:}5\) mole ratio. What is the mass percent of Na\(_2\)C\(_2\)O\(_4\) in the sample most nearly?$px$ WHERE id = 'c5c725d6-d93f-42b5-b434-d38b2d53fd92';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is B because synthesis consumes \(\text{ATP}\) and \(\text{NADPH}\) to build a fatty acid, while oxidation breaks that same fatty acid back down. If both ran at once on the same molecules, the cell would spend energy building and then immediately dismantling fatty acids with no net gain — a futile cycle. Physical separation (cytosol vs. matrix) plus reciprocal regulation ensures only one direction runs at a time, so the invested \(\text{ATP}\) and \(\text{NADPH}\) are not wasted.

(Choice B) is correct: separation and reciprocal control prevent the simultaneous synthesize-and-burn futile cycle that would waste \(\text{ATP}\) and \(\text{NADPH}\).

(Choice A) is incorrect: running both at full speed does not increase energy yield; it dissipates energy, because the product of one pathway is the substrate the other destroys.

(Choice C) is incorrect: synthesis consumes \(\text{NADPH}\) and does not produce \(\text{NADH}\) to hand off; oxidation generates its own \(\text{NADH}\) and \(\text{FADH}_2\), so there is no such direct hand-off from synthesis.

(Choice D) is incorrect: the two pathways use different enzymes (e.g., cytosolic fatty-acid synthase vs. the matrix beta-oxidation enzymes), so shared active sites are not the reason for compartmentation.

This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must reason from the energy cost of each pathway to why simultaneous operation would be wasteful.$px$ WHERE id = 'c5d2f729-7b9c-4df2-be44-aa8ff0eb01b0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is C because a disulfide bond (cystine) is a covalent \(-\text{S}-\text{S}-\) crosslink formed by oxidation of two cysteine thiols, and it is one of the R-group interactions that stabilize a protein's overall three-dimensional tertiary structure. A reducing agent such as DTT (or beta-mercaptoethanol) cleaves these disulfides, regenerating free thiols (cystine to cysteine); disulfides can be cleaved by reducing agents such as beta-mercaptoethanol, dithiothreitol, or tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphine (TCEP), and disulfide bonds within individual chains and between them stabilize both tertiary and quaternary structures. Removing a stabilizing covalent crosslink unfolds the native tertiary structure, and loss of three-dimensional shape means the protein may no longer be functional. (Choice A) is incorrect: reducing agents act on the \(-\text{S}-\text{S}-\) bond, not on the backbone amide bonds; denaturation occurs without altering the primary sequence, so peptide bonds remain intact. (Choice B) is incorrect because it reverses the redox direction—DTT is a reducing agent that breaks disulfides, whereas disulfide formation requires oxidation in the presence of oxygen; no new bridges form. (Choice C) is correct: the reducing agent reduces the cystine crosslinks, removing a covalent stabilizer of the tertiary fold and causing it to unfold. (Choice D) is incorrect because the backbone hydrogen bonds that define secondary structure (alpha-helices, beta-sheets) are not the target of a thiol-reducing agent; DTT acts specifically on the cysteine-derived disulfide, an R-group covalent bond, not on backbone H-bonds. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the chemistry of a reducing agent to predict its effect on the bond it targets and on the level of protein structure that bond stabilizes.$px$ WHERE id = 'c614f7cf-c7f1-4ccd-80d5-42cb26721b3e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Oxidative Phosphorylation.'

The answer is D because a block at Complex III cuts the chain in two. Electrons keep arriving from the NADH-linked substrate but cannot pass Complex III, so everything upstream — \(\text{NADH}\), Complex I, and coenzyme Q — accumulates in the reduced state. Nothing new reaches cytochrome c or Complex IV, so those downstream carriers give up their electrons to \(\text{O}_2\) and remain oxidized, and because no electrons reach Complex IV, \(\text{O}_2\) consumption stops.

(Choice D) is correct: upstream carriers back up (stay reduced) and downstream carriers stay oxidized, with \(\text{O}_2\) use halted — the signature of a mid-chain block.

(Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the pattern. Carriers downstream of the block cannot be re-reduced because no electrons cross Complex III, so cytochrome c and Complex IV stay oxidized, not reduced.

(Choice B) is incorrect: coenzyme Q hands electrons to Complex III, not directly to Complex IV; there is no bypass, so respiration cannot continue.

(Choice C) is incorrect: with the chain severed, electrons cannot reach Complex IV at all, so \(\text{O}_2\) consumption falls to zero rather than rising, and downstream carriers stay oxidized.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, the student must reason from the location of the block to the redox state on each side and to the fate of \(\text{O}_2\) consumption.$px$ WHERE id = 'c6a5c421-f7ef-4b3d-b93f-e161bf51281b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is C because the stem states the barrier explicitly: the inner membrane has no transporter for acetyl-CoA, but the cell can move the two carbons across if it first packages them into a molecule the membrane will accept. Citrate is exactly that vehicle: it crosses on a carrier, and once in the cytosol \(\text{ATP}\)-citrate lyase splits it back into acetyl-CoA and oxaloacetate. The problem to be solved is transport, so the correct reason is that citrate is membrane-permeant and acetyl-CoA is not.

(Choice C) is correct: citrate solves a transport problem — it crosses the inner membrane where acetyl-CoA cannot, delivering the two-carbon unit to the cytosol where it is released as acetyl-CoA again.

(Choice A) is incorrect: the cleavage of citrate by ATP-citrate lyase actually consumes ATP; the shuttle is not a way to import extra energy, and fatty-acid synthesis draws its reducing power from NADPH, not from citrate's bonds.

(Choice B) is incorrect: forming citrate is a condensation, not an oxidation, and acetyl-CoA's problem is that it lacks a transporter, not that it is dangerously reactive in the matrix.

(Choice D) is incorrect: the actual two-carbon donor to fatty-acid synthase is malonyl-CoA (made from cytosolic acetyl-CoA), not citrate; citrate is only the carrier that regenerates cytosolic acetyl-CoA before synthesis begins.

This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must reason from the stated transport barrier to why an intermediate carrier is required.$px$ WHERE id = 'c75e3358-5e7a-4634-bd05-32c6af2b4b83';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is C because the diaphragm is skeletal muscle innervated by the phrenic nerve and therefore receives voluntary somatic motor commands (allowing the breath-hold) as well as involuntary drive from the respiratory centers, which are driven largely by central chemoreceptors sensing rising CO2 and falling pH; as breath-holding drives CO2 up, that involuntary drive grows until it overrides conscious suppression and forces inspiration. (Choice C) is correct: dual innervation of a skeletal muscle explains both the conscious override (voluntary suspension) and its temporary nature (involuntary chemoreceptor-driven command eventually wins). (Choice A) is incorrect: the diaphragm is skeletal, not smooth muscle, and it has no intrinsic pacemaker; its rhythm is imposed by the brainstem respiratory centers, not a myogenic pacemaker. (Choice B) is incorrect: the diaphragm is not cardiac muscle and is not autorhythmic — if it were truly uninterruptible, a voluntary breath-hold would be impossible in the first place, contradicting the scenario. (Choice D) is incorrect: the hold does not end because of muscle fatigue from staying relaxed (relaxation requires no sustained effort); it ends because the involuntary chemoreceptor-driven inspiratory command overrides the voluntary suppression, so 'purely voluntary control' cannot account for the involuntary gasp. This item tests the skill of reasoning about the physiological consequences of the diaphragm's dual (somatic and autonomic) neural control.$px$ WHERE id = 'c7d365bb-7cf8-454f-bc43-434a6dda36fc';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' In a long bone, each epiphysis meets the diaphysis at the metaphysis, a narrow region that in a growing bone contains the epiphyseal (growth) plate—a layer of hyaline cartilage. Longitudinal growth occurs as this cartilage proliferates and is progressively replaced by osseous tissue added to the diaphysis; when growth stops in early adulthood (~18–21 years) the plate ossifies into the epiphyseal line. The scenario describes a hyaline-cartilage region present in the child but ossified in the adult—the epiphyseal plate at the metaphysis. (Choice A) is correct: the epiphyseal plate is the site of longitudinal bone growth, so destroying its hyaline cartilage in a still-growing child would most directly stunt the bone's ability to lengthen. (Choice B) is incorrect: red-marrow hematopoiesis occurs in the spongy bone of the epiphysis, a distinct region; the growth plate's cartilage does not produce blood cells, so damaging it would not primarily halt blood-cell production. (Choice C) is incorrect: yellow marrow stores triglycerides within the medullary cavity of the diaphysis, not in the epiphyseal-plate cartilage, so fat storage would be unaffected by damage to this region. (Choice D) is incorrect: tendons and ligaments attach at the periosteum covering the bone's outer surface, an entirely separate structure from the internal growth-plate cartilage. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the functional roles of long-bone regions to predict the consequence of damaging the epiphyseal plate rather than simply recalling a region's name.$px$ WHERE id = 'c7ded429-8cc8-49fd-a5ac-5c327277e068';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Impulse Propagation & Synapses'. The answer is D because the action potential and its \(\text{Na}^+\)-driven depolarization still occur, but with no extracellular \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) available to enter when the voltage-gated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels open, the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-triggered fusion of synaptic vesicles cannot happen, so no transmitter is exocytosed. The text states that when an action potential reaches the terminal, voltage-gated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels open and 'Calcium ions entering the cell initiate a signaling cascade that causes... synaptic vesicles... to fuse with the presynaptic membrane' (Biology 2e), and that 'The \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) facilitates the merging of the vesicle with the presynaptic membrane so that the neurotransmitter is released through exocytosis' (A&P 2e). Because the stem fixes the presynaptic spike as normal but the postsynaptic response as absent, the only step that depends on the missing variable—extracellular \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) influx—is vesicle fusion and release, pinpointing that as the break. (Choice A) is wrong because action potential conduction into the terminal depends on voltage-gated \(\text{Na}^+\) channels, not on extracellular \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\), and the stem explicitly reports the presynaptic spike is unchanged. (Choice B) is wrong because neurotransmitter-receptor binding is a key-and-lock event across the cleft that does not require extracellular \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\), and here the block is presynaptic, before any transmitter is released. (Choice C) is wrong because clearance by degradation or reuptake is a later step that acts on already-released transmitter; the response is lost because nothing is released, not because nothing is cleared. (Choice D) is correct because removing the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) that must enter to trigger fusion abolishes exocytosis while leaving the upstream electrical signal intact. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires interpreting an experimental manipulation, holding the unchanged presynaptic action potential as a control, and reasoning from the absent postsynaptic response to localize the defect to the specific \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-dependent fusion step.$px$ WHERE id = 'c806c158-0701-4277-90ce-229b1819cae8';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Principles of Bioenergetics.'

The answer is B because the operative free energy is \(\Delta G = \Delta G^{\circ\prime} + RT\ln Q\). Here Q = [B]/[A] = 0.010/1.0 = 0.010, so \(RT\ln Q = (8.314\,\text{J/mol·K})(298\,\text{K})(-4.6) \approx -11{,}400\ \text{J/mol} = -11.4\ \text{kJ/mol}\) (convert J → kJ before combining), giving \(\Delta G = +7.5 + (-11.4) = -3.9\) kJ/mol — negative, so the reaction runs forward in the cell despite the positive \(\Delta G^{\circ\prime}\), because the product is held far below 1 M.

(Choice A) is incorrect: +7.5 kJ/mol is \(\Delta G^{\circ\prime}\) itself and ignores the \(RT\ln Q\) correction supplied by the low product concentration.

(Choice C) is incorrect: +18.9 kJ/mol adds the magnitude of the \(RT\ln Q\) term to \(\Delta G^{\circ\prime}\) (7.5 + 11.4), giving that term the wrong sign; because Q < 1, its logarithm is negative.

(Choice D) is incorrect: −11.4 kJ/mol is only the \(RT\ln Q\) term and omits the +7.5 kJ/mol standard contribution.

(Choice B) is correct: combining the standard term (+7.5 kJ/mol) with the concentration term (−11.4 kJ/mol) yields −3.9 kJ/mol, a spontaneous forward process.

This item is Skill 4: you must compute Q from the given concentrations, evaluate \(RT\ln Q\), and combine it with \(\Delta G^{\circ\prime}\) to obtain the actual \(\Delta G\).$px$ WHERE id = 'c81877f6-e1de-4853-aade-af1abb66b71a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'

The answer is A because two independent injuries compound each other. First, carbon monoxide binds the heme Fe²⁺ sixth coordination site with far higher affinity than dioxygen, so the 40% of sites it occupies simply cannot carry O₂ — a loss of carrying capacity. Second, the reported drop in \(P_{50}\) (from 26 mm Hg toward a lower value) signals a left-shift of the dissociation curve for the sites still carrying dioxygen: lower \(P_{50}\) means higher O₂ affinity. Because tissue O₂ delivery depends on hemoglobin *releasing* (unloading) O₂ at the low \(pO_2\) of respiring tissue, a left-shifted, tighter-binding population unloads a smaller fraction of what it carries. So the deliverable O₂ falls by more than the 40% capacity loss alone — the unloading defect stacks on top of the capacity defect. This is why 40% carboxyhemoglobin is far more dangerous than simply losing 40% of red cells.

(Choice A) is correct: it captures both mechanisms — reduced capacity plus impaired unloading from the higher-affinity (lower \(P_{50}\)) remaining sites — which is exactly what produces delivery loss greater than 40%.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it accounts only for the capacity loss and assumes the remaining sites behave normally. It ignores the stated fall in \(P_{50}\); a left-shifted curve unloads less O₂ at tissue tensions, so the true deficit exceeds 40%.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses the physiological consequence of higher affinity. Tighter binding helps *loading* in the lungs but hurts *unloading* in tissues, and the plasma dissolved-O₂ pool is far too small to matter for delivery. Preserved or improved delivery is the opposite of what a left-shift causes.

(Choice D) is incorrect: dissolved O₂ at a PaO₂ of 95 mm Hg carries only a tiny amount of oxygen (roughly 0.3 mL/dL) relative to hemoglobin-bound O₂; a normal PaO₂ cannot compensate for lost hemoglobin capacity and impaired release, which is precisely why the patient is hypoxic despite normal PaO₂.

As a Skill 2 problem, success depends not on recalling that CO binds hemoglobin, but on integrating two facts — occupancy lowers capacity and a lower \(P_{50}\) means tighter binding — and predicting their combined effect on the unloading step that actually delivers O₂ to tissue.$px$ WHERE id = 'c824e2ef-23e2-413a-a4ed-3debb5e8cd59';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. The length of any period equals the total number of orbitals filled across that row, with each orbital holding two electrons (and thus accommodating two elements). Period 6 fills the \(6s\) subshell (1 orbital, 2 elements), then the \(4f\) subshell (7 orbitals, 14 elements — the lanthanides), then the \(5d\) subshell (5 orbitals, 10 elements), and finally the \(6p\) subshell (3 orbitals, 6 elements). Summing gives \(2 + 14 + 10 + 6 = 32\), so period 6 contains 32 elements, matching choice D. The key structural feature distinguishing period 6 from period 4 is the appearance of an \(f\) subshell: once \(n = 6\), the \(4f\) orbitals finally drop low enough in energy to fill, adding 14 elements that periods 4 and 5 lack. (Choice A) Counting 8 elements reflects only the \(ns\) and \(np\) subshells (\(6s\) plus \(6p\), giving \(2 + 6 = 8\)), the width of a short representative-element period like period 2 or 3. It omits the \(5d\) transition metals and the \(4f\) lanthanides that also fill across period 6. (Choice B) Counting 18 elements reflects the \(6s\), \(5d\), and \(6p\) subshells (\(2 + 10 + 6 = 18\)), which is the correct width of period 4 or period 5 but not period 6. It omits the \(4f\) subshell, whose 14 lanthanides are the defining addition that makes period 6 longer than the rows above it. (Choice C) Counting 50 elements adds a second \(f\) subshell to the tally (\(2 + 14 + 14 + 10 + 6 = 46\), or treating two \(f\) blocks as \(2 + 14 + 14 + 10 + 6\) and rounding the count upward), incorporating an \(f\) subshell that does not fill until period 7 (the \(5f\) actinides). Only one \(f\) subshell, the \(4f\), fills during period 6. Skill 1$px$ WHERE id = 'c844949a-1eb3-4967-8773-087ba088655c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' At saturation the Michaelis-Menten model gives v = Vmax = kcat[E]0, a regime that is zero order with respect to S and first order in E, since for all reactions Vmax = kcat[E]0. Because Vmax scales directly with the total enzyme concentration, the plateau rate is set by how much enzyme is present and its turnover number, not by substrate. (Choice A) is correct: since Vmax = kcat[E]0, doubling [E]0 doubles Vmax and therefore raises the saturated velocity; the rate at saturation is limited by enzyme quantity and turnover, which more enzyme directly increases. (Choice B) is incorrect: this repeats the very instinct the stem shows fails — above saturation the rate is zero order in substrate, so when [S] >> KM, v = Vmax regardless of further additions; a student who thinks 'more reactant always speeds a reaction' picks this. (Choice C) is incorrect: lowering \(K_m\) raises affinity and shifts the low-[S] region of the curve, but it does not change the plateau; Vmax is independent of \(K_m\) and equals kcat[E]0, so at already-saturating [S] a tighter-binding substrate does not raise the ceiling. (Choice D) is incorrect: Vmax is not an immutable property of the enzyme species — it depends on how much enzyme is in the tube (Vmax = kcat[E]0), so treating it as a fixed absolute ceiling ignores its dependence on [E]0. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship Vmax = kcat[E]0 to reason that, at saturating substrate, only increasing enzyme (not substrate or affinity) can raise the observed velocity.$px$ WHERE id = 'c887e8b1-1426-42c7-89a0-d264e9e046d3';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' Crossing an organism that shows the dominant trait with a homozygous recessive individual is a test cross, used to reveal whether the dominant-expressing parent is homozygous or heterozygous. The wrinkled parent is \(rr\) and can donate only an \(r\) allele, so every offspring's phenotype is determined by the allele it receives from the round-seeded parent. (Choice B) is correct: because roughly half the offspring are wrinkled \((rr)\), the round parent must have supplied an \(r\) allele to about half its gametes and an \(R\) allele to the other half; in a test cross, a heterozygous dominant parent produces a 1:1 ratio of heterozygotes to recessive homozygotes in the F1, so the round parent is \(Rr\). (Choice A) is incorrect: a homozygous dominant \((RR)\) parent could donate only \(R\) alleles, so a test cross would yield all round-seeded offspring and no wrinkled ones (every F1 would be a heterozygote expressing the dominant trait); the appearance of wrinkled offspring rules this out. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reverses the logic by attributing the recessive offspring to the wrong parent — a wrinkled-seeded plant is by definition homozygous recessive \((rr)\) and cannot carry a hidden \(R\), so the wrinkled offspring trace to a recessive allele from the round parent, not from the wrinkled parent. (Choice D) is incorrect: incomplete dominance would produce heterozygotes with an intermediate phenotype rather than two distinct classes, and the stem specifies complete dominance; the clean split into fully round and fully wrinkled offspring is exactly the Mendelian test-cross outcome, not blending. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the logic of the test cross and the law of segregation to reason backward from an observed offspring ratio to the unknown parental genotype.$px$ WHERE id = 'c899414a-ca16-43c2-b6ca-a636f802c88b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' Enzyme shape determines substrate binding and catalysis: in one mode of binding the reactant fits the active site with no change to enzyme shape, while in the other, contact between the reactant and the protein changes the protein's shape so that the closed form can position residues to convert substrate to product. The crystallography shows enzyme X's site is unchanged on binding (a rigid, preformed site) and enzyme Y's site closes around the substrate, bringing catalytic residues to the reacting bond — the flexible closure is not decorative but is what assembles the functional catalytic geometry. (Choice A) is correct: because enzyme Y's catalytic residues only reach the scissile bond in the closed, substrate-induced conformation, a mutation that locks the site open would leave those residues out of position; the enzyme could still bind substrate yet fail to catalyze efficiently, which is exactly what the data (catalytic contacts form only in the closed form) predict. (Choice B) is incorrect: nothing in the data ties an unchanging active site to faster turnover, and the reasoning is spurious — a rigid site is not inherently faster, and enzyme Y's induced closure is what organizes catalysis, so a rigid enzyme is not guaranteed to out-run a flexible one. (Choice C) is incorrect: a shape-changing site does not abolish specificity; selectivity comes from which ligands can productively trigger and stabilize the closed, catalytically competent conformation, not merely from whether the site can move, so 'closes around almost anything' does not follow. (Choice D) is incorrect: it inverts the roles — the enzyme that reshapes around its substrate (Y) is the one whose closure enables transition-state chemistry, whereas the rigid enzyme (X) binds without a shape change; the option assigns transition-state dependence to the wrong enzyme. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structural criterion distinguishing the two binding modes to crystallographic data and predict a functional consequence of disrupting a substrate-induced conformational change.$px$ WHERE id = 'c8f6b0d4-23af-4a8e-a01d-f6c9ed2e25c5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is A because in cold conditions the smooth muscle around the nasal and tracheal capillary beds contracts, narrowing the vessels (vasoconstriction); this pulls warm blood away from the airway surface so that less core heat is transferred to the cold inhaled air and lost, helping the body conserve heat and maintain core temperature. (Choice A) is correct: constriction reduces surface blood flow, which minimizes conductive heat loss to inhaled air — the appropriate heat-conserving response. (Choice B) is incorrect: it correctly identifies the goal (limiting heat loss) but pairs it with dilation; dilation would increase warm blood at the surface and worsen heat loss, so the vascular direction is wrong. (Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly identifies constriction but misstates the purpose — the goal in cold is to conserve, not maximize, heat transfer to inhaled air. (Choice D) is incorrect: dilation with evaporative cooling describes a heat-dissipating (e.g., panting/hot-environment) response, the opposite of what cold demands. This question requires predicting a vascular response from an environmental scenario and connecting it to its thermoregulatory purpose.$px$ WHERE id = 'c9702c7b-3f13-436b-94f1-f15c903133fd';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).' Cilia, flagella, and microvilli are all apical projections, but they differ in length, number, and function. Flagella are long, hair-like structures that enable an entire cell to move, and a cell bears just one flagellum or a few. Cilia, by contrast, are short, hair-like structures that, when present, occur in large numbers extending along nearly the plasma membrane's entire surface, and they move substances along the cell's outer surface — for example, the cilia lining the cells of the respiratory tract trap particulate matter and move it toward the nostrils. Microvilli are non-motile fingerlike projections that increase the surface area available for absorption. (Choice A) is correct: dozens of short projections spanning the whole membrane match the length and number of cilia, and in the respiratory tract cilia are motile and beat to move overlying material (mucus) along a fixed cell's surface. (Choice B) is incorrect: flagella are long and occur singly or in small numbers, which contradicts the "dozens of short" projections observed, and flagella move the whole cell rather than sweeping surface material. (Choice C) is incorrect: the identity (cilia) and structure fit, but the function is wrong — airway cilia move the mucus along a stationary cell; they do not propel the anchored epithelial cell through its own mucus. (Choice D) is incorrect: microvilli are indeed short and numerous, so the structural description alone does not rule them out, but they are non-motile surface-area amplifiers and cannot beat to move mucus, so they are not the airway's motile clearance structure. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall how cilia, flagella, and microvilli differ in length, number, and function.$px$ WHERE id = 'c9cc067c-b8bb-4023-8257-7d23fd06ec01';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$In the basal layer of a patient's epidermis, a keratin mutation prevents keratin intermediate filaments from attaching to desmosomes, although the desmosomes themselves still link neighboring keratinocytes. When the patient's skin is subjected to mechanical shearing, blisters form within this layer. Which prediction about the affected tissue most directly follows from the loss of this keratin–desmosome attachment?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is B because keratin intermediate filaments bear tension and maintain cell shape, and by anchoring to desmosomes they link each keratinocyte's internal scaffold to its neighbors so that mechanical stress is shared across the whole epidermal sheet; severing that anchorage means tension can no longer pass cell to cell, so stressed cells rupture and the layer splits into a blister. The text states that intermediate filaments "bear tension, thus maintaining the cell's shape, and anchor the nucleus and other organelles in place" and identifies keratin as the intermediate-filament protein "that strengthens your hair, nails, and the skin's epidermis." Intermediate filaments are purely structural and have no role in movement or in resisting compression, so the consequence of losing their tension-distributing attachment is mechanical failure under shear rather than collapse, crawling, or loss of adhesion machinery. (Choice A) Wrong: intermediate filaments are purely structural and generate no contractile force — the text says they "have no role in cell movement," so cells do not actively crawl apart; this confuses keratin with actin/myosin. (Choice B) Correct: keratin intermediate filaments bear tension and, via desmosome anchorage, distribute mechanical load between adjacent cells; without that link the cells cannot share stress, rupture, and the layer separates. (Choice C) Wrong: resisting compression is the role of microtubules ("they help the cell resist compression"), not of tension-bearing intermediate filaments, so the predicted compressive collapse misassigns the failure mode. (Choice D) Wrong: the stem specifies the desmosomes themselves still link neighboring keratinocytes — only the keratin attachment to them is lost — so the defect is the filament–desmosome connection, not detachment of the desmosomes from the membrane. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the tension-bearing and cell-anchoring role of intermediate filaments to predict the tissue-level mechanical consequence of breaking their attachment to desmosomes.$px$ WHERE id = 'ca3fcef6-4de3-4e1f-b3cc-1b1a4aaeda6e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is C because absolute configuration for a sugar is assigned by inspecting the hydroxyl group on the highest-numbered (last) chiral carbon in the Fischer projection. In the structure shown, that hydroxyl is drawn on the left-hand side. By convention, a sugar whose last chiral center has its OH pointing to the left is designated L (a useful memory aid: the OH on the Last chiral carbon points to the Left). The structure shown is therefore L-glyceraldehyde.

(Choice C) is correct: With the reference OH on the left of the Fischer projection, the molecule matches the definition of an L sugar, related to L-glyceraldehyde.

(Choice B) is incorrect: D is assigned when the OH on the last chiral carbon points to the right in the Fischer projection. Reading the projection shown reveals the OH on the opposite side, so D is the enantiomer of the structure depicted, not the structure itself.

(Choice A) is incorrect: The lowercase \(l\) (levorotatory) label describes the direction a compound rotates plane-polarized light and must be measured experimentally; it does not denote absolute configuration and cannot be read off a Fischer projection. Uppercase D/L and lowercase d/l are independent designations, so this choice confuses optical rotation with structural assignment.

(Choice D) is incorrect: R is a Cahn–Ingold–Prelog (CIP) descriptor based on ranking substituent priorities, not the D/L system biochemists use for sugars. The question asks for the D/L assignment, so an R/S label is the wrong nomenclature category even though it also describes a chiral center.

This is a Skill 1 (Knowledge of Scientific Concepts) question because it requires recognizing the definition of the D/L convention and applying the last-chiral-carbon rule directly to the hydroxyl position shown in the figure.$px$ WHERE id = 'ca4d6a6c-bcbb-4e4d-b5e9-843aadaa148a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is B because the recycling loop described normally lets the liver reuse bile salts many times, so it spends very little cholesterol replacing them. The drug breaks that loop: by binding bile salts and forcing their excretion, it stops them from returning to the liver. To keep enough bile salts for fat digestion, the liver must now synthesize large amounts of new bile salt, and because bile salts are made from cholesterol, this ongoing replacement draws down the body's cholesterol. Diverting cholesterol into bile-salt synthesis (and the liver's compensatory uptake of cholesterol from the blood to supply it) lowers blood cholesterol.

(Choice B) is correct: interrupting recycling forces continual de novo bile-salt synthesis from cholesterol, consuming cholesterol and lowering its blood level.

(Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the direction of the effect. Although bile salts are made from cholesterol, trapping and excreting bile salts removes that cholesterol-derived material from the body rather than conserving it, so the drug lowers, not raises, cholesterol.

(Choice C) is incorrect: bile-salt turnover and cholesterol are directly linked because bile salts are synthesized from cholesterol. Forcing more bile-salt synthesis necessarily draws on cholesterol, so the pathways are not independent.

(Choice D) is incorrect: the sequestrant binds bile salts, not cholesterol molecules directly. The cholesterol-lowering effect is indirect — through increased bile-salt synthesis — not through the drug scooping up dietary cholesterol.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, this asks you to trace the consequence of interrupting enterohepatic recycling to its effect on cholesterol, rather than recall the drug class.$px$ WHERE id = 'ca6c184a-b498-47da-9812-f720b5ac43bf';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the electrode where reduction happens is by definition the cathode, and silver ion is being reduced and plated onto the copper pendant (\(\text{Ag}^+ + e^- \rightarrow \text{Ag}(s)\)), so the pendant is the cathode. The silver bar, where neutral silver is oxidized to \(\text{Ag}^+\) to replenish the solution (\(\text{Ag} \rightarrow \text{Ag}^+ + e^-\)), is the anode. In every electrochemical cell — galvanic or electrolytic — oxidation occurs at the anode and reduction at the cathode, and electrons in the external wire always travel from the anode to the cathode; here that means from the silver bar to the copper pendant. The defining contrast is the driving force, not the direction of electron travel: a galvanic cell pushes electrons from anode to cathode spontaneously (\(E_{\text{cell}} > 0\), \(\Delta G < 0\)), whereas this electrolytic cell is non-spontaneous and the external power source forces electrons in that same anode-to-cathode direction (\(E_{\text{cell}} < 0\), \(\Delta G > 0\)). (Choice A) keeps the correct anode-to-cathode electron direction and the driven-rather-than-spontaneous contrast, but it mislabels the dissolving silver bar as the site of reduction; the silver bar is being oxidized (\(\text{Ag} \rightarrow \text{Ag}^+ + e^-\)), so it is the anode, and reduction takes place at the pendant instead. (Choice B) places reduction at the silver bar, but the silver bar is being oxidized (it dissolves), so it is the anode, not the cathode; it also reverses the universal anode-to-cathode electron direction, mistakenly assuming non-spontaneity flips which way electrons move through the wire. (Choice C) correctly identifies the copper pendant as the site of reduction (the cathode) but then sends electrons from the pendant to the silver bar — that is cathode-to-anode, the reverse of the actual anode-to-cathode flow; electrons always arrive at the cathode, never leave it. Skill 2$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"Reduction occurs at the silver bar, and electrons travel from the silver bar to the pendant through the wire — the same anode-to-cathode direction as in a galvanic cell, but driven rather than spontaneous.","label":"A"},{"text":"Reduction occurs at the silver bar, and electrons travel from the pendant to the silver bar through the wire — the opposite direction from a galvanic cell because the process is non-spontaneous.","label":"B"},{"text":"Reduction occurs at the copper pendant, and electrons travel from the pendant to the silver bar through the wire — the reverse of a galvanic cell because the power source drives current backward.","label":"C"},{"text":"Reduction occurs at the copper pendant, and electrons travel from the silver bar to the pendant through the wire — the same anode-to-cathode direction as in a galvanic cell, but here driven by the power source rather than occurring spontaneously.","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'ca8524df-58da-44c0-b407-953be845d56d';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is C because the T-tubules carry the action potential into the cell's interior, where it triggers the opening of calcium channels in the membrane of the adjacent sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR). The T-tubules conduct the action potential deep into the cell, which triggers the opening of calcium channels in the membrane of the adjacent SR, causing \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) to diffuse out of the SR and into the sarcoplasm. T-tubules are periodic invaginations in the sarcolemma that exist precisely so the action potential can reach the membrane of the SR, ensuring the depolarization can get close to the SR deep inside a fiber up to 100 μm wide. If the T-tubules are detached, the depolarization cannot propagate to the SR in the interior, so the SR \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels never open and the deep myofibrils receive no \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) signal. (Choice A) Acetylcholine release occurs upstream at the neuromuscular junction from the motor neuron's axon terminal, before any action potential travels along the sarcolemma, so it is not the event the T-tubules deliver the impulse to trigger. (Choice B) Reuptake of \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) into the SR is part of relaxation—\(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) ions are pumped back into the SR—which happens when signaling stops, the reverse of the release event a T-tubule action potential triggers. (Choice C) is correct because the arrival of the action potential at the SR via the T-tubules is the direct trigger for SR \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channel opening and \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) efflux into the sarcoplasm. (Choice D) \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binding to troponin is a downstream consequence that can only occur after \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) has already been released from the SR into the sarcoplasm, so it is not the event the T-tubule directly triggers. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that T-tubules conduct the action potential into the fiber interior to trigger SR calcium-channel opening.$px$ WHERE id = 'caeef42e-9d57-47d0-9a2b-d7df6ccb0774';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' During an all-out sprint, muscles regenerate \(\text{ATP}\) mostly through creatine phosphate and anaerobic glycolysis, depleting phosphate reserves and accumulating lactic acid. Intense muscle activity produces an oxygen debt — the amount of oxygen needed to compensate for \(\text{ATP}\) produced without oxygen — and after exercise stops, oxygen intake stays elevated to restore \(\text{ATP}\) and creatine phosphate levels and to convert lactic acid back to pyruvic acid (and, in the liver, into glucose or glycogen), which is why breathing rate remains high even after activity ends. (Choice A) is correct: the sustained \(\text{O}_2\) uptake repays the oxygen debt by rebuilding the creatine phosphate and \(\text{ATP}\) energy reserves and metabolizing the lactic acid that built up during the anaerobic effort. (Choice B) is incorrect: the stem states her muscles are relaxed and no longer contracting, so ongoing high-rate contraction cannot be the reason; oxygen demand during recovery reflects metabolic repair, not continued mechanical work. (Choice C) is incorrect: pyruvic acid is converted to lactic acid precisely when \(\text{O}_2\) is absent, allowing glycolysis to continue anaerobically; this conversion does not consume oxygen, so it cannot explain the elevated post-exercise \(\text{O}_2\) uptake. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{NAD}^+\) is regenerated in the cytosol by converting pyruvic acid to lactic acid during anaerobic glycolysis, a process that requires no oxygen; oxygen debt instead pays back reserves and clears lactate rather than sustaining anaerobic glycolysis. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of oxygen debt to a novel post-exercise scenario and infer that continued oxygen use serves metabolic recovery, information the scenario does not state directly.$px$ WHERE id = 'cbd1db92-9aa2-4810-ad6a-b1731d045ef4';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is C because gene flow is the movement of alleles in and out of a population due to the migration of individuals or gametes; this variable flow of individuals not only changes the population's genetic structure, but can also introduce new genetic variation to populations in different geological locations and habitats. Because migrants carry alleles from the source island into the recipient island, gene flow adds new variation to the recipient pool while making the two geographically separated groups' allele frequencies more similar — ongoing gene flow keeps an allele's frequency at one end of a range similar to its frequency at the other end, whereas blocking that flow lets the frequencies gradually become increasingly different. (Choice A) describes the founder effect, in which the genetic structure of a small group that starts a new population reflects only the alleles the founding individuals happened to carry; here migrants are joining an existing population, not founding a new one. (Choice B) describes genetic drift, the effect of chance, which removes alleles by random sampling rather than by directed migration. (Choice C) is correct: migration of individuals moves alleles into the recipient pool, adding variation and homogenizing allele frequencies between the separated islands. (Choice D) describes the bottleneck effect, in which a catastrophe such as an earthquake suddenly wipes out a large portion of the gene pool, reducing variation rather than adding it. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of gene flow to predict its specific effect on a recipient population's alleles and distinguish it from drift, founder, and bottleneck effects.$px$ WHERE id = 'cc8f0bbd-696a-4497-ac86-bd0cdbd8602e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Ions in Solutions (GC).' An ionic compound must be electrically neutral, so the total positive charge contributed by the cations must exactly cancel the total negative charge contributed by the anions; the subscripts in the formula are the smallest whole-number ratio of ions that achieves this balance. Here the aluminum ion carries a \(3+\) charge and the polyatomic sulfate ion carries a \(2-\) charge, so the ratio must reconcile a \(3+\) with a \(2-\). (Choice A) is correct: the least common multiple of \(3\) and \(2\) is \(6\), so two \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) ions supply \(6+\) and three \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\) ions supply \(6-\), giving the neutral \(2:3\) ratio written as \(\text{Al}_2(\text{SO}_4)_3\). (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{AlSO}_4\) pairs the ions \(1:1\), which leaves a net charge of \((3+)+(2-)=1+\); this is the error of assuming every cation and anion combine one-to-one without checking the magnitudes of their charges. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{Al}_3(\text{SO}_4)_2\) results from using each ion's own charge magnitude as its own subscript — putting the aluminum charge of \(3\) on aluminum and the sulfate charge of \(2\) on sulfate instead of crossing the charges over; this gives \(3(3+)=9+\) against \(2(2-)=4-\), a net \(5+\) rather than a neutral compound. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{Al}_2\text{SO}_4\) balances two \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) ions \((6+)\) against a single sulfate treated as \(6-\); it reflects a misremembered sulfate charge of \(3-\) (or a failure to include the three sulfate units needed) and is not neutral for the actual \(2-\) sulfate ion. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle of charge neutrality, together with the known charges of the aluminum and sulfate ions, to derive the correct whole-number combining ratio rather than recall a memorized formula.$px$ WHERE id = 'ccba48c1-a286-4950-a372-ba134d8e4336';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).' The three cytoskeletal fiber types have distinct jobs: microfilaments (actin) rapidly depolymerize and reform to let a cell change shape and crawl, and they act as the track for the motor protein myosin; microtubules (tubulin) are tracks for vesicle transport and chromosome separation; and intermediate filaments (keratin among them) are purely structural and have no role in cell movement. (Choice A) is correct: the text states that actin filaments "can depolymerize (disassemble) and reform quickly, thus enabling a cell to change its shape and move," and it names white blood cells that "move to an infection site and phagocytize the pathogen" as the example; actin also "serves as a track for the movement of a motor protein we call myosin," so actin fits both the crawling and the myosin-track clues. (Choice B) is incorrect: tubulin builds microtubules, which can also disassemble and reform quickly and do serve as tracks, but their motor partners are kinesin and dynein—not myosin—and they drive vesicle transport and chromosome movement, not the amoeboid crawling of the cell's leading edge. (Choice C) is incorrect: keratin is a genuine intermediate-filament protein, but the text is explicit that intermediate filaments "have no role in cell movement" and are "purely structural," so they cannot be the fibers that power crawling. (Choice D) is incorrect: myosin is the motor named in the stem, not a structural monomer—it walks along the actin track rather than polymerizing into it, so it cannot be the fiber being assembled and disassembled at the leading edge. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinct functional roles of the three cytoskeletal fibers to identify which monomer both drives rapid crawling and serves as the myosin track.$px$ WHERE id = 'ccbf2e7c-8275-4dff-be3a-236e45dc96db';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is B because the panel—low insulin with high glucagon and epinephrine, elevated free fatty acids and glycerol, rising lactate, and near-normal blood glucose—describes the counter-regulatory, fuel-mobilizing state of prolonged exercise: adipose lipolysis feeds fatty acids to working muscle whose own glycogen is falling, while the liver holds blood glucose steady through glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis using Cori-cycle lactate and glycerol.

(Choice B) is correct: it links the low-insulin, high-glucagon-and-epinephrine signal to hepatic glucose output and adipose lipolysis, and to the endurance muscle's shift toward fat oxidation, integrating hormones, fuels, and tissues.
(Choice A) is incorrect: the brain cannot oxidize fatty acids because they do not cross the blood-brain barrier, and red blood cells have no mitochondria, so neither tissue can burn the elevated free fatty acids.
(Choice C) is incorrect: insulin is low, not rising, so the anabolic, GLUT4-recruiting, glycogen-storing fed pattern is exactly the state being suppressed here.
(Choice D) is incorrect: skeletal muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase and cannot release free glucose, and the Cori cycle runs the other way—muscle exports lactate to the liver, which makes the glucose.

This item rewards Skill 4 (data-based reasoning) by requiring the student to read the hormone and metabolite panel, identify the exercise fuel-mobilization state, and integrate the fuel choices of liver, adipose, and muscle.$px$ WHERE id = 'cd61bfd0-75bf-4d9e-ac87-e722d1059683';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. A noble-gas-core abbreviation replaces the inner electrons of an atom with the symbol of the noble gas that terminates the period directly above the element. Arsenic lies in period 4, so its core is the configuration of argon, the element closing period 3; the bracket \([\text{Ar}]\) therefore stands for the \(18\) core electrons \(1s^2 2s^2 2p^6 3s^2 3p^6\), and the electrons written explicitly after the bracket (\(4s^2 3d^{10} 4p^3\)) are those added across period 4. Because argon ends period 3, the row immediately above arsenic, choice A correctly identifies both the row and the role of the bracketed electrons. (Choice B) Argon does not close period 4; period 4 ends at krypton, and the noble-gas core never represents the element's own row, since the explicitly written \(4s^2 3d^{10} 4p^3\) electrons—not the bracket—are arsenic's outermost electrons. (Choice C) A noble gas marks the end, not the start, of a period; the element opening period 4 is potassium, an alkali metal, and the bracketed symbol denotes a filled noble-gas configuration that precedes the \(4s\) electrons rather than launching the new row. (Choice D) Although the bracket does represent core electrons from the end of period 3, the \(3d^{10}\) subshell is written explicitly outside the bracket and is filled during period 4; it is not part of the \([\text{Ar}]\) core, which contains only the \(18\) electrons through \(3p^6\). Skill 1$px$ WHERE id = 'cd8b8a67-1f58-4478-94e1-15c8449e75d4';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is A because the lung is normally held expanded not by any glue but by a pressure difference: the intrapleural space is subatmospheric, so the pressure across the lung wall (alveolar minus intrapleural, the transpulmonary pressure) is positive and holds the lung distended against its own inward elastic recoil; puncturing the parietal membrane lets air enter until intrapleural pressure rises to atmospheric — the same as alveolar pressure — collapsing that transpulmonary gradient to zero and letting the unopposed recoil pull the lung inward while the freed chest wall springs out. (Choice A) is correct: equalizing intrapleural and alveolar pressure abolishes the positive transpulmonary pressure, so nothing counters the lung's elastic recoil and it collapses. (Choice B) is incorrect: although a thin serous film does provide cohesion and couples the two pleurae, the film is not a rigid glue holding the lung open against recoil; the decisive factor is the loss of the subatmospheric pressure gradient, not evaporation of fluid, and pleural fluid is not simply expelled through a small air channel. (Choice C) is incorrect: with the airway open, alveolar pressure equilibrates with the atmosphere and does not exceed the newly atmospheric intrapleural pressure, so no net inward push is generated across the lung wall — the collapse is driven by the lung's own recoil, not by air pressing it in. (Choice D) is incorrect: the lung's elastic fibers were already under recoil tension before the injury and were being held stretched by the transpulmonary gradient; the fibers do not gain new tension, they simply act unopposed once that gradient vanishes. This item exercises the skill of reasoning about how disrupting a physiological pressure relationship changes a system's mechanical behavior.$px$ WHERE id = 'cda1f18a-2e5c-4ff6-8c71-d8ee08d77dd4';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because panting is rapid, shallow breathing: each small tidal volume mostly ventilates the moist conducting airways (nose, pharynx, trachea) rather than the alveoli. Water evaporating from these warm, vascularized surfaces carries away large amounts of heat, while the shallow depth means the extra air exchanged is largely dead-space air that does not appreciably increase alveolar gas exchange. Because alveolar ventilation barely rises, CO2 elimination is not greatly boosted, so arterial CO2 and pH stay near normal — the animal cools without driving a respiratory alkalosis.
(Choice D) is correct: high-frequency, low-depth airflow maximizes evaporative cooling over upper-airway surfaces while keeping alveolar ventilation — and thus CO2 loss — nearly constant.
(Choice A) is incorrect: it wrongly claims a large rise in alveolar ventilation; that would blow off substantial CO2 and raise pH (respiratory alkalosis), contradicting the stable blood gases observed. Panting deliberately stays shallow to avoid this.
(Choice B) is incorrect: deep, forceful breaths would maximize alveolar gas exchange and CO2 offloading, disturbing blood pH, which is the opposite of what panting achieves; cooling comes from evaporation over airway surfaces, not from warming exhaled gas via deep alveolar contact.
(Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses the vascular response — effective heat loss requires vasodilation of airway capillary beds to deliver warm blood to evaporating surfaces, not constriction, and constriction would impair rather than aid cooling.
This question tests the skill of reasoning from an observed physiological outcome (heat loss with stable blood gases) back to the mechanism that produces it.$px$ WHERE id = 'cdb06068-ce65-45fd-a2f9-228cc32c42d7';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A researcher measures the plasma half-lives of two hormones in a patient: Hormone X falls to half its concentration in about 1 minute, while Hormone Y takes 60–90 minutes. Which conclusion about Hormone Y is best supported?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Mechanisms of Hormone Action.' The answer is C because a half-life of 60–90 minutes marks Hormone Y as a lipid-derived (steroid) hormone: because steroids are hydrophobic and cannot dissolve freely in water-based blood, they travel bound to a transport protein, and this carrier-bound state shields them from degradation and renal filtration, extending the half-life far beyond that of amino acid–derived hormones. Hormone X's ~1-minute half-life matches an amino acid–derived hormone such as epinephrine, which circulates free and is cleared almost immediately. (Choice C) is correct: transport-protein binding is precisely what protects a steroid hormone from rapid clearance and produces the long half-life observed for Hormone Y, so the 60–90 minute value is best explained by carrier-bound circulation. (Choice B) is incorrect: pre-secretion vesicular storage is characteristic of water-soluble peptide/amine hormones, and where a hormone is stored before release does not govern how quickly it is cleared once it is already circulating in plasma. (Choice A) is incorrect: signaling through a cell-surface (membrane) receptor is the hallmark of water-soluble hormones like the rapidly cleared Hormone X, whereas the long-lived steroid pattern of Hormone Y instead points toward intracellular receptor action. (Choice D) is incorrect: rapid enzymatic degradation would shorten, not lengthen, the half-life, so it describes the ~1-minute clearance of Hormone X and directly contradicts the 60–90 minute persistence of Hormone Y. Skill 2 is assessed because the student must translate quantitative clearance data into hormone class and then reason from that class to the underlying transport mechanism.$px$ WHERE id = 'cdd11f36-d328-4aa6-ae8a-1a867b20e041';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).' Prokaryotes are classified by shape into cocci (spherical), bacilli (rod-shaped), and spirilli (spiral-shaped), and their small size and high surface-area-to-volume ratio let ions and organic molecules diffuse across the membrane quickly. For a fixed volume, a sphere has the smallest possible surface area, so a cell can raise its surface area, and thus its diffusive exchange, by becoming flat or thin and elongated. When uptake is diffusion-limited across the membrane, more membrane surface per unit cytoplasm means a higher potential uptake rate. (Choice A) is correct: because a sphere minimizes surface area for a given volume, drawing that same volume out into a rod increases the membrane area available per unit of cytoplasm, raising the surface-area-to-volume ratio and supporting a higher diffusion-limited uptake rate at equal transporter density. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the geometry — a sphere does not maximize but rather minimizes surface area for a fixed volume, giving the coccus the lowest surface-area-to-volume ratio of the two shapes. (Choice C) is incorrect: the two cells were specified to have the same cytoplasmic volume, so no extra storage volume exists; moreover, when uptake is diffusion-limited the constraint is membrane surface area, not internal storage capacity. (Choice D) is incorrect: at equal transporter density the total uptake still scales with membrane surface area, and shape changes that area relative to volume, so morphology does affect the diffusion-limited uptake rate. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the surface-area-to-volume principle to two bacterial morphologies and infer the functional consequence for diffusive nutrient uptake, rather than recall a shape name.$px$ WHERE id = 'ce101963-869e-42fc-a214-91840c341225';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.' In the Lewis model, an acid is any species that accepts a pair of electrons to form a coordinate covalent bond, while a base is the species that donates that pair; identifying the acid therefore requires locating the species with a vacant orbital rather than reading off charges or electronegativities. (Choice B) is correct: boron in \(\text{BF}_3\) has only six valence electrons (three \(\text{B}\text{-}\text{F}\) bonds and no lone pair), so it has an empty valence orbital and can accept the lone pair on nitrogen, making \(\text{BF}_3\) the electron-pair acceptor and thus the Lewis acid. (Choice A) is incorrect: nitrogen in \(\text{NH}_3\) does hold the lone pair used to form the bond, but donating that pair is the defining behavior of a Lewis base, so this feature identifies \(\text{NH}_3\) as the base, not the acid. (Choice C) is incorrect: it also mislabels \(\text{NH}_3\) as the acid, and although nitrogen is more electronegative than boron, pulling electron density is not the same as accepting a lone pair into an empty orbital, and \(\text{NH}_3\) is in fact the donor. (Choice D) is incorrect: it reaches the right species but for a false reason—fluorine, not boron, is the electronegative element in \(\text{BF}_3\); boron acts as the acid because of its empty orbital and incomplete octet, not because it is electron-rich. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the Lewis acid-base definition to a novel reactant pair and reason from each species' electron structure to assign the acceptor role.$px$ WHERE id = 'ce64d613-c297-4b9c-87e6-899a504dc5d7';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Ions in Solutions (GC).' The naming system for oxyanions (per-, -ate, -ite, hypo-) fixes only the relative ordering of oxygen atoms within a single element's series; it does not set a universal oxygen count or a universal charge, because those depend on the central atom. As the text notes, the number of oxygens tied to a given suffix is not consistent across elements—nitrate is \(\text{NO}_3^{-}\) while sulfate is \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\)—so each familiar ion's formula and charge must be known. Phosphate, the "-ate" ion of phosphorus, is \(\text{PO}_4^{3-}\). (Choice A) is correct: phosphate carries four oxygen atoms and a 3- charge, the value the AAMC lists among the familiar ions to know; nothing in the stem's sulfate or chlorate data supplies this, so it must be recalled and applied. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{PO}_3^{-}\) copies chlorate's three-oxygen, 1- pattern onto phosphorus, the exact "all -ate ions match" error the stem flags; phosphate is not the phosphorus analog of chlorate. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{PO}_4^{2-}\) borrows sulfate's 2- charge while keeping four oxygens, mixing a correct oxygen count with the wrong charge for the phosphorus center. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{PO}_4^{-}\) keeps four oxygens but assigns a 1- charge, treating every "-ate" ion as singly charged the way chlorate and nitrate are. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that oxyanion suffixes fix only relative oxygen ordering—not absolute oxygen count or charge—and then recall the correct formula and charge of a familiar ion whose value the stem never provides.$px$ WHERE id = 'ce88e2db-676d-4e25-985b-94e2ebde1317';

COMMIT;
